[08:50:47] {"type":"thinking_delta","payload":{"delta":"This is a warm resume in REPAIR mode... wait, actually it says \"Repair mode (ACTIVE)\" but the failure was a tool-call validation error, and no EPICS-SPEC exists yet. Let me check: the previous run was interrupted at the grep call (my grep failed schema validation repeatedly). No _progress.json exists for this step. The PRD exists on disk. So actually nothing of the epics work was done — the run died before writing anything.\n\nThe \"Repair mode ACTIVE\" block says: if Repair feedback opens with `## REPAIR TARGET` directive, follow that. There's no repair feedback content naming a rejected artifact — the failure text is just the grep error. So effectively this is a CONTINUE/BUILD: no EPICS-SPEC exists yet, so I need to do the full BUILD.\n\nPlan:\n1. Check guidance file (new path).\n2. Write _progress.json FIRST ACTION to output folder.\n3. Read PRD-SPEC-pd-20261027-calc001.md in full (§12 delegated exploration — but I can read inline; it's ~51KB, ~maybe 800 lines).\n4. Read the backlog template reference: .coda/skills/planning-epics/references/backlog-template.md.\n5. Read project-brief.md for MVP filter (Step 5).\n6. Generate EPICS-SPEC + EPICS-AUDIT section by section.\n7. Write sidecar.\n\nSESSION_ID: The PRD used \"pd-20261027-calc001\". I need a SESSION_ID for filenames. The run id is 1793188233471-kgm30pt. Hmm — what SESSION_ID to use? The skill says EPICS-SPEC-{SESSION_ID}.md. Previous PRD used session id \"pd-20261027-calc001\". I could use the stepwise run id. Let me check `stepwise session show` maybe. Simpler: use the run id \"1793188233471-kgm30pt\"? That's ugly but unique. Actually the PRD-SPEC used \"pd-20261027-calc001\" which looks like a session id minted by the prior skill. For consistency I could mint \"epi-20261028-calc001\"? Zero-invention... The skill says SESSION_ID format is defined in §1 (Session Identity) but that's only for code-development family. For this skill, SESSION_ID goes in