[08:50:56] {"type":"tool_end","payload":{"toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-b607537241e58571","toolName":"read","result":"<file path=\"C:\\IA Trainings\\CalcService\\artifacts\\outputs\\product-definition\\prd\\PRD-SPEC-pd-20261027-calc001.md\" lines=\"589\" start=\"1\" end=\"400\" returned=\"400\" has_more=\"true\" truncated=\"false\">\n     1|# Math Operations Microservice (Calc-Service) — PRD Spec\n     2|version: 1.0.1\n     3|session: pd-20261027-calc001\n     4|mode: SYNTHESIZE\n     5|date: 2026-10-28\n     6|language: en\n     7|status: draft\n     8|contract_format: structured\n     9|content_hash: 25c7814b3af060433c725d5e0d92674538e4af564960493da328ea935e63f93a\n    10|---\n    11|\n    12|## project_info\n    13|client_name: Internal platform organization (client organization not named in source inputs; service built for internal platform teams)\n    14|project_name: Math Operations Microservice (Calc-Service)\n    15|project_description: A lightweight, stateless microservice that provides a centralized, deterministic mathematical computation capability for the platform, externalizing calculation logic from client applications to enable consistency, reuse, and controlled evolution of numerical behavior across the ecosystem.\n    16|\n    17|---\n    18|\n    19|## scope\n    20|\n    21|### in_scope\n    22|- Standard arithmetic operations: addition, subtraction, multiplication, division | source: project-brief.md §2 Core Capabilities; discovery-transcript.md §01:30 | status: complete\n    23|- Advanced operations: exponentiation, roots, trigonometric functions | source: project-brief.md §2 Core Capabilities; discovery-transcript.md §01:30 | status: complete\n    24|- High-precision internal computation producing deterministic outputs suitable for financial and scientific contexts | source: project-brief.md §2 Precision Strategy; discovery-transcript.md §03:00 | status: complete\n    25|- Optional history tracking of calculation metadata for traceability and diagnostics, as lightweight local persistence that must not impact response delivery | source: project-brief.md §2 History Tracking; discovery-transcript.md §04:30, §11:00 | status: complete\n    26|- Calculate operation API: `POST /api/v1/math/calculate` accepting operands and operation, returning result, operation type, and timestamp | source: project-brief.md §4 API Contract; discovery-transcript.md §07:30 | status: complete\n    27|- History retrieval capability providing recent calculation records for diagnostics and auditing | source: project-brief.md §4 History Retrieval | status: complete\n    28|- Clear, fail-fast error handling for invalid input (division by zero, unsupported operation) | source: discovery-transcript.md §10:00 | status: complete\n    29|- Extensibility: new operations added without breaking existing consumers | source: discovery-transcript.md §01:30 | status: complete\n    30|- Stateless microservice deployment model: independent deployment, horizontally scalable computation layer, no user session ownership | source: project-brief.md §3 Architectural Positioning | status: complete\n    31|\n    32|### out_of_scope\n    33|- Batch calculations | source: discovery-transcript.md §07:30 (\"Maybe later, not day one\") | status: complete\n    34|- Expression evaluation (e.g., `\"5 + (3 * 2)\"`) | source: project-brief.md §2 Future Capability (Planned); discovery-transcript.md §07:30 (\"not required now\") | status: complete\n    35|- Workflow integration with automation agents | source: project-brief.md §2 Future Capability (Planned) | status: complete\n    36|- Event-based history instead of database persistence | source: project-brief.md §2 Future Capability (Planned) | status: complete\n    37|- Multi-instance history consistency | source: discovery-transcript.md §11:00 (\"Not required now\") | status: complete\n    38|- Regulatory-grade audit persistence of every calculation | source: discovery-transcript.md §04:30, §11:00 (\"Not necessarily every one... more diagnostic than regulatory\") | status: complete\n    39|\n    40|### research_methodology\n    41|- Derived from project brief (v1.1) analysis and one 15-minute discovery transcript with the product owner; no primary user interviews, surveys, or meeting recordings provided; meetings and documentation input folders were empty | source: project-brief.md; discovery-transcript.md | status: complete\n    42|\n    43|---\n    44|\n    45|## personas\n    46|\n    47|### P-01: Backend Service Developer\n    48|- type: primary\n    49|- description: Developer building backend services that need consistent mathematical logic and currently face duplicated calculation implementations across systems | source: project-brief.md §3 Primary Consumers; discovery-transcript.md §00:00, §06:00\n    50|- goals: consume a single, consistent math engine instead of embedding calculation logic in each service; get deterministic results for the same input | source: discovery-transcript.md §00:00, §03:00\n    51|- pain_points: calculations spread across frontend, backend, and reports; formulas changing break things in different places; rounding differences between systems | source: discovery-transcript.md §00:00, §03:00\n    52|- technical_proficiency: high (builds and integrates backend services) | status: assumption [ASM-01]\n    53|- usage_frequency: not stated in inputs\n    54|- demographics: backend service developer; high technical proficiency; works in a multi-team platform organization with frontend, backend, and reporting teams | source: discovery-transcript.md §00:00, §06:00 | status: assumption [ASM-01]\n    55|- prioritization_rationale: backend services are the first and primary consumers named by the product owner (\"Backend services first\") | source: discovery-transcript.md §06:00 | status: complete\n    56|- status: complete\n    57|- source: project-brief.md §3; discovery-transcript.md §00:00, §03:00, §06:00\n    58|\n    59|### P-02: Automation Workflow Engineer\n    60|- type: secondary\n    61|- description: Engineer building automation workflows that call the service synchronously as part of pipelines, including validating outputs inside pipelines | source: project-brief.md §3 Primary Consumers; discovery-transcript.md §06:00, §12:00\n    62|- goals: reliable, lightweight synchronous calculations inside automated workflows; potential throughput spikes when automation kicks in | source: discovery-transcript.md §06:00, §09:00, §12:00\n    63|- pain_points: not stated in inputs\n    64|- technical_proficiency: high (builds automation workflows) | status: assumption [ASM-01]\n    65|- usage_frequency: not stated in inputs\n    66|- demographics: automation/workflow engineer; high technical proficiency; operates within platform automation teams | source: discovery-transcript.md §06:00, §12:00 | status: assumption [ASM-01]\n    67|- status: complete\n    68|- source: project-brief.md §3; discovery-transcript.md §06:00, §09:00, §12:00\n    69|\n    70|### P-03: AI Agent / AI-Driven Process Operator\n    71|- type: secondary\n    72|- description: Operator or maintainer of AI agents performing deterministic calculations as part of AI-driven processes | source: project-brief.md §3 Primary Consumers (\"AI agents performing deterministic calculations\"); discovery-transcript.md §06:00 (\"Possibly AI agents\")\n    73|- goals: deterministic calculations from an AI-driven process with predictable, repeatable results | source: project-brief.md §3; discovery-transcript.md §03:00\n    74|- pain_points: not stated in inputs\n    75|- technical_proficiency: not stated in inputs\n    76|- usage_frequency: not stated in inputs\n    77|- demographics: AI agent operator; technical proficiency not stated in inputs; organizational context not stated in inputs | status: assumption [ASM-01]\n    78|- status: complete\n    79|- source: project-brief.md §3; discovery-transcript.md §06:00\n    80|\n    81|### P-04: Reporting Pipeline Engineer\n    82|- type: secondary\n    83|- description: Engineer building reporting pipelines that consume the calculation service for consistent numerical behavior in reports | source: project-brief.md §3 Primary Consumers; discovery-transcript.md §00:00 (calculations spread across \"even reports\")\n    84|- goals: consistent numerical results across reporting outputs | source: discovery-transcript.md §00:00, §03:00\n    85|- pain_points: rounding differences between systems affecting reports | source: discovery-transcript.md §03:00\n    86|- technical_proficiency: high (builds reporting pipelines) | status: assumption [ASM-01]\n    87|- usage_frequency: not stated in inputs\n    88|- demographics: reporting pipeline engineer; high technical proficiency; part of platform reporting function | source: discovery-transcript.md §00:00 | status: assumption [ASM-01]\n    89|- status: complete\n    90|- source: project-brief.md §3; discovery-transcript.md §00:00, §03:00\n    91|\n    92|---\n    93|\n    94|## functional_requirements\n    95|\n    96|### FR-01: Execute standard arithmetic operations (addition, subtraction, multiplication, division)\n    97|- priority: must_have\n    98|- source: project-brief.md §2 Standard Operations; discovery-transcript.md §01:30 (\"Basic arithmetic first — add, subtract, multiply, divide. That covers most cases.\")\n    99|- status: complete\n   100|- design_note: not stated in inputs\n   101|- depends_on: [none identified]\n   102|- acceptance_criteria:\n   103|  - FR-01-AC-01: given: the service is running | when: a calculate request is submitted with two numeric operands and operation `ADD` | then: the response returns the sum of the operands as `resultValue`, with `operationType` set to `ADD` and a `timestamp` present\n   104|  - FR-01-AC-02: given: the service is running | when: a calculate request is submitted with operation `SUBTRACT` | then: the response returns the difference of operand1 minus operand2 as `resultValue`\n   105|  - FR-01-AC-03: given: the service is running | when: a calculate request is submitted with operation `MULTIPLY` | then: the response returns the product of the operands as `resultValue`\n   106|  - FR-01-AC-04: given: the service is running | when: a calculate request is submitted with operation `DIVIDE` and a non-zero divisor | then: the response returns the quotient of the operands as `resultValue`\n   107|\n   108|### FR-02: Execute advanced operations (exponentiation, roots, trigonometric functions)\n   109|- priority: should_have\n   110|- source: project-brief.md §2 Advanced Operations; discovery-transcript.md §01:30 (\"powers, square roots, some trigonometry\")\n   111|- status: complete\n   112|- design_note: not stated in inputs\n   113|- depends_on: [FR-01]\n   114|- acceptance_criteria:\n   115|  - FR-02-AC-01: given: the service is running | when: a calculate request is submitted with an exponentiation operation | then: the response returns the exponentiation result as `resultValue`\n   116|  - FR-02-AC-02: given: the service is running | when: a calculate request is submitted with a root operation | then: the response returns the root result as `resultValue`\n   117|  - FR-02-AC-03: given: the service is running | when: a calculate request is submitted with a trigonometric operation | then: the response returns the trigonometric result as `resultValue`\n   118|  - FR-02-AC-04: given: the service is running | when: a calculate request is submitted with an advanced operation not yet supported by the service | then: the service returns a clear error identifying the unsupported operation\n   119|\n   120|### FR-03: Produce deterministic, high-precision results\n   121|- priority: must_have\n   122|- source: project-brief.md §2 Precision Strategy, §5 Determinism; discovery-transcript.md §03:00 (\"deterministic outputs for the same input\", \"We don't want floating-point surprises\")\n   123|- status: complete\n   124|- design_note: high-precision internal computation is the stated mechanism to reduce rounding inconsistencies; the concrete precision policy (decimal digits, rounding mode) is not stated in inputs | status: pending\n   125|- depends_on: [FR-01]\n   126|- acceptance_criteria:\n   127|  - FR-03-AC-01: given: any supported operation | when: the same request is submitted repeatedly | then: the response returns the identical `resultValue` every time\n   128|  - FR-03-AC-02: given: two different client systems submit the same calculation | when: both responses are compared | then: both return the identical `resultValue` with no rounding differences between systems\n   129|\n   130|### FR-04: Expose a calculate operation via `POST /api/v1/math/calculate`\n   131|- priority: must_have\n   132|- source: project-brief.md §4 API Contract; discovery-transcript.md §07:30 (\"Simple. Send operands and operation, get result. No complex payloads.\")\n   133|- status: complete\n   134|- design_note: request/response field names `operand1`, `operand2`, `operation`, `resultValue`, `operationType`, `timestamp` are taken verbatim from the brief's conceptual contract | status: assumption [ASM-02]\n   135|- depends_on: [FR-01]\n   136|- acceptance_criteria:\n   137|  - FR-04-AC-01: given: the service is running | when: a request is sent to `POST /api/v1/math/calculate` with body `{\"operand1\": 10.5, \"operand2\": 5.0, \"operation\": \"ADD\"}` | then: the response contains `resultValue: 15.5`, `operationType: \"ADD\"`, and a `timestamp` value\n   138|  - FR-04-AC-02: given: the service is running | when: a calculate request is submitted | then: the response contains only the simple result payload (result, operation type, timestamp) with no complex payload structure\n   139|\n   140|### FR-05: Return clear, fail-fast errors for invalid input\n   141|- priority: must_have\n   142|- source: discovery-transcript.md §10:00 (\"Clear errors. Division by zero, unsupported operation — that kind of thing.\" / \"Strict validation? Yes. Fail fast.\")\n   143|- status: complete\n   144|- design_note: not stated in inputs\n   145|- depends_on: [FR-01]\n   146|- acceptance_criteria:\n   147|  - FR-05-AC-01: given: the service is running | when: a calculate request is submitted with operation `DIVIDE` and operand2 equal to zero | then: the service returns a clear error identifying division by zero and does not return a numeric result\n   148|  - FR-05-AC-02: given: the service is running | when: a calculate request is submitted with an operation value the service does not support | then: the service returns a clear error identifying the unsupported operation\n   149|  - FR-05-AC-03: given: the service is running | when: a calculate request is submitted with missing or non-numeric operands | then: the service rejects the request with a clear validation error before any computation\n   150|\n   151|### FR-06: Record calculation history as optional, auxiliary local persistence\n   152|- priority: could_have\n   153|- source: project-brief.md §2 History Tracking; discovery-transcript.md §04:30 (\"we want the ability to log calculations when needed — mainly debugging and traceability\"), §11:00 (\"persistence is auxiliary\")\n   154|- status: complete\n   155|- design_note: implemented as lightweight local persistence via an embedded database abstraction; persistence can be replaced without changing the API contract | status: assumption [ASM-03]\n   156|- depends_on: [FR-01]\n   157|- acceptance_criteria:\n   158|  - FR-06-AC-01: given: history tracking is enabled | when: a calculation is executed | then: calculation metadata is recorded for traceability and diagnostics\n   159|  - FR-06-AC-02: given: history tracking is enabled | when: a calculation is executed | then: the history write does not delay or block delivery of the calculation response\n   160|  - FR-06-AC-03: given: history tracking is disabled or not configured | when: a calculation is executed | then: the calculation still executes and returns a result normally\n   161|\n   162|### FR-07: Calculation must succeed even if history persistence fails\n   163|- priority: must_have\n   164|- source: project-brief.md §5 Reliability; discovery-transcript.md §04:30 (\"If persistence fails, should the calculation still return?\" / \"Yes, always.\"), §15:00 (\"Persistence must never block calculation\")\n   165|- status: complete\n   166|- design_note: not stated in inputs\n   167|- depends_on: [FR-06]\n   168|- acceptance_criteria:\n   169|  - FR-07-AC-01: given: history persistence is failing or unavailable | when: a valid calculate request is submitted | then: the calculation still succeeds and returns the correct result to the caller\n   170|\n   171|### FR-08: Provide history retrieval of recent calculation records\n   172|- priority: could_have\n   173|- source: project-brief.md §4 History Retrieval (\"Provides recent calculation records for diagnostics and auditing\")\n   174|- status: complete\n   175|- design_note: the retrieval endpoint path and response shape are not stated in inputs | status: pending\n   176|- depends_on: [FR-06]\n   177|- acceptance_criteria:\n   178|  - FR-08-AC-01: given: history tracking is enabled and calculations have been executed | when: a history retrieval request is submitted | then: the service returns recent calculation records for diagnostics and auditing\n   179|\n   180|### FR-09: Support extensibility so new operations are added without breaking existing consumers\n   181|- priority: must_have\n   182|- source: discovery-transcript.md §01:30 (\"Definitely. New operations will be added without breaking existing consumers.\")\n   183|- status: complete\n   184|- design_note: not stated in inputs\n   185|- depends_on: [FR-01]\n   186|- acceptance_criteria:\n   187|  - FR-09-AC-01: given: existing consumers are integrated against the calculate operation | when: a new operation is added to the service | then: existing consumers continue to receive correct results for previously supported operations with no contract change\n   188|\n   189|### FR-10: Operate as a stateless, independently deployable, horizontally scalable service\n   190|- priority: must_have\n   191|- source: project-brief.md §3 Architectural Positioning (\"Independent deployment\", \"Horizontally scalable computation layer\", \"No user session ownership\")\n   192|- status: complete\n   193|- design_note: stateless design is the stated mechanism enabling horizontal scaling | status: assumption [ASM-04]\n   194|- depends_on: [none identified]\n   195|- acceptance_criteria:\n   196|  - FR-10-AC-01: given: multiple service instances are deployed | when: calculate requests are distributed across instances | then: each request is served independently with no user session ownership and identical results for identical inputs\n   197|  - FR-10-AC-02: given: the service is deployed | when: the service is deployed or scaled independently of other platform components | then: calculation behavior is unchanged\n   198|\n   199|---\n   200|\n   201|## non_functional_requirements\n   202|\n   203|### NFR-01: Standard operations respond at millisecond-level latency\n   204|- category: performance\n   205|- target: response time at millisecond level for standard operations [PENDING: exact numeric threshold] \n   206|- source: project-brief.md §5 Latency (\"Millisecond-level response time for standard operations\"); discovery-transcript.md §09:00 (\"It should feel instant. This isn't heavy computation.\")\n   207|- status: pending\n   208|- design_note: not stated in inputs\n   209|\n   210|### NFR-02: Same input always produces the same result (determinism)\n   211|- category: compliance\n   212|- target: 100% of repeated identical requests return the identical resultValue\n   213|- source: project-brief.md §5 Determinism; discovery-transcript.md §03:00\n   214|- status: complete\n   215|- design_note: not stated in inputs\n   216|\n   217|### NFR-03: Calculation succeeds even when history persistence fails\n   218|- category: availability\n   219|- target: 100% of valid calculate requests return a result when history persistence is unavailable\n   220|- source: project-brief.md §5 Reliability; discovery-transcript.md §04:30, §15:00\n   221|- status: complete\n   222|- design_note: behavioral guarantee mirrored as FR-07; retained here as the availability-level statement of the same guarantee | status: assumption [ASM-05]\n   223|\n   224|### NFR-04: Stateless design supports horizontal scaling\n   225|- category: scalability\n   226|- target: service capacity scales horizontally by adding instances with no change to calculation behavior [PENDING: numeric concurrency/volume target]\n   227|- source: project-brief.md §5 Scalability, §3 Architectural Positioning; discovery-transcript.md §09:00 (\"Possibly if automation kicks in, but calculations are lightweight\")\n   228|- status: pending\n   229|- design_note: not stated in inputs\n   230|\n   231|### NFR-05: Observability readiness for future metrics and tracing integration\n   232|- category: usability\n   233|- target: [PENDING: nfr_target — no measurable observability target stated in inputs]\n   234|- source: project-brief.md §5 Observability readiness (\"Designed for future metrics and tracing integration\")\n   235|- status: pending\n   236|- design_note: not stated in inputs\n   237|\n   238|### NFR-06: Security and access control for the calculation API\n   239|- category: security\n   240|- target: [PENDING: nfr_target — no security requirement stated in inputs]\n   241|- source: not stated in inputs\n   242|- status: pending\n   243|- design_note: not stated in inputs\n   244|\n   245|### NFR-07: API usability for internal consumers\n   246|- category: usability\n   247|- target: [PENDING: nfr_target — no usability/accessibility standard stated in inputs]\n   248|- source: discovery-transcript.md §07:30 (\"Simple. Send operands and operation, get result.\"), §13:30 (\"it must be simple or teams won't use it\")\n   249|- status: pending\n   250|- design_note: not stated in inputs\n   251|\n   252|---\n   253|\n   254|## kpis\n   255|\n   256|### KPI-01: Upstream system integration adoption\n   257|- target: at least 1 upstream system successfully integrated\n   258|- measurement: count of upstream systems integrated with the calculate operation\n   259|- timeframe: not stated in inputs\n   260|- source: project-brief.md §7 Success Criteria (\"Service successfully integrated by at least one upstream system\")\n   261|- status: complete\n   262|\n   263|### KPI-02: Deterministic test coverage of all operations\n   264|- target: 100% of supported operations covered by deterministic unit tests\n   265|- measurement: deterministic unit test coverage report across all operations\n   266|- timeframe: not stated in inputs\n   267|- source: project-brief.md §7 Success Criteria (\"Deterministic unit test coverage for all operations\")\n   268|- status: complete\n   269|\n   270|### KPI-03: History persistence validation\n   271|- target: optional history persistence validated\n   272|- measurement: validation run confirming history persistence works without impacting response delivery\n   273|- timeframe: not stated in inputs\n   274|- source: project-brief.md §7 Success Criteria (\"Optional history persistence validated\")\n   275|- status: complete\n   276|\n   277|### KPI-04: API contract documentation for internal reuse\n   278|- target: API contract documented for internal reuse\n   279|- measurement: published API contract documentation available to internal consumers\n   280|- timeframe: not stated in inputs\n   281|- source: project-brief.md §7 Success Criteria (\"API contract documented for internal reuse\")\n   282|- status: complete\n   283|\n   284|### KPI-05: Reduction of duplicated math logic across teams\n   285|- target: [PENDING: numeric target — success criterion stated qualitatively (\"Teams stop duplicating math logic\")]\n   286|- measurement: not stated in inputs\n   287|- timeframe: not stated in inputs\n   288|- source: discovery-transcript.md §14:30 (\"Teams stop duplicating math logic\")\n   289|- status: pending\n   290|\n   291|### KPI-06: Result consistency across systems\n   292|- target: [PENDING: numeric target — success criterion stated qualitatively (\"Results become consistent\")]\n   293|- measurement: not stated in inputs\n   294|- timeframe: not stated in inputs\n   295|- source: discovery-transcript.md §14:30 (\"Results become consistent\")\n   296|- status: pending\n   297|\n   298|### KPI-07: Calculation traceability availability\n   299|- target: [PENDING: numeric target — success criterion stated qualitatively (\"We can trace calculations when something looks wrong\")]\n   300|- measurement: not stated in inputs\n   301|- timeframe: not stated in inputs\n   302|- source: discovery-transcript.md §14:30 (\"We can trace calculations when something looks wrong\")\n   303|- status: pending\n   304|\n   305|---\n   306|\n   307|## jtbd\n   308|\n   309|### JTBD-01: core_functional\n   310|- situation: when a backend service, report, or workflow needs a calculation performed, the calculation logic is currently duplicated across frontend, backend, and reporting systems | source: discovery-transcript.md §00:00\n   311|- motivation: obtain a consistent, correct numerical result from a single authoritative math engine instead of maintaining formula logic in each consumer | source: discovery-transcript.md §00:00; project-brief.md §1 Business Objective\n   312|- outcome: the caller receives a deterministic result for the operation requested, identical to what any other system would receive for the same input | source: discovery-transcript.md §03:00; project-brief.md §5 Determinism\n   313|- source: discovery-transcript.md §00:00, §03:00; project-brief.md §1, §5\n   314|- status: complete\n   315|\n   316|### JTBD-02: related\n   317|- situation: when a formula or numerical behavior changes, each system that embedded its own calculation logic must be found and fixed individually, and changes break things in different places | source: discovery-transcript.md §00:00\n   318|- motivation: evolve numerical behavior in one controlled place so changes propagate consistently across the ecosystem | source: project-brief.md §1 (\"controlled evolution of numerical behavior\"); discovery-transcript.md §00:00\n   319|- outcome: calculation logic is updated once in the central service and all consumers immediately use the same updated behavior without breaking existing consumers | source: discovery-transcript.md §01:30; project-brief.md §1\n   320|- source: discovery-transcript.md §00:00, §01:30; project-brief.md §1\n   321|- status: complete\n   322|\n   323|### JTBD-03: related\n   324|- situation: when a calculation result looks wrong, an engineer needs to understand what the service computed and why | source: discovery-transcript.md §04:30, §14:30\n   325|- motivation: trace and debug calculations through recorded calculation metadata without coupling persistence to computation | source: discovery-transcript.md §04:30 (\"mainly debugging and traceability\"); project-brief.md §2 History Tracking\n   326|- outcome: recent calculation records are available for diagnostics and auditing, and retrieving them never degrades the calculation itself | source: project-brief.md §2 History Tracking, §4 History Retrieval; discovery-transcript.md §04:30, §11:00\n   327|- source: discovery-transcript.md §04:30, §11:00, §14:30; project-brief.md §2, §4\n   328|- status: complete\n   329|\n   330|### JTBD-04: related\n   331|- situation: when an automated workflow or AI-driven process runs, it needs lightweight deterministic calculations performed synchronously as part of the pipeline | source: discovery-transcript.md §06:00, §09:00, §12:00\n   332|- motivation: validate outputs and perform computations inside pipelines using a stable, reusable computation service rather than bespoke logic | source: discovery-transcript.md §12:00 (\"Potentially as part of automated workflows — like validating outputs inside pipelines\"); project-brief.md §1\n   333|- outcome: automation workflows and AI agents obtain instant, deterministic calculation results as a foundational building block for AI-assisted workflows | source: discovery-transcript.md §06:00, §09:00; project-brief.md §6\n   334|- source: discovery-transcript.md §06:00, §09:00, §12:00; project-brief.md §1, §6\n   335|- status: complete\n   336|\n   337|---\n   338|\n   339|## traceability\n   340|\n   341|| FR | fr_name | JTBD | jtbd_name | direction | status |\n   342||----|---------|------|-----------|-----------|--------|\n   343|| FR-01 | Execute standard arithmetic operations (addition, subtraction, multiplication, division) | JTBD-01 | core_functional | forward | mapped |\n   344|| FR-02 | Execute advanced operations (exponentiation, roots, trigonometric functions) | JTBD-01 | core_functional | forward | mapped |\n   345|| FR-03 | Produce deterministic, high-precision results | JTBD-01 | core_functional | forward | mapped |\n   346|| FR-04 | Expose a calculate operation via `POST /api/v1/math/calculate` | JTBD-01 | core_functional | forward | mapped |\n   347|| FR-05 | Return clear, fail-fast errors for invalid input | JTBD-01 | core_functional | forward | mapped |\n   348|| FR-06 | Record calculation history as optional, auxiliary local persistence | JTBD-03 | related | forward | mapped |\n   349|| FR-07 | Calculation must succeed even if history persistence fails | JTBD-03 | related | forward | mapped |\n   350|| FR-08 | Provide history retrieval of recent calculation records | JTBD-03 | related | forward | mapped |\n   351|| FR-09 | Support extensibility so new operations are added without breaking existing consumers | JTBD-02 | related | forward | mapped |\n   352|| FR-10 | Operate as a stateless, independently deployable, horizontally scalable service | JTBD-04 | related | forward | mapped |\n   353|| — | — | JTBD-01 | core_functional | reverse | mapped |\n   354|| — | — | JTBD-02 | related | reverse | mapped |\n   355|| — | — | JTBD-03 | related | reverse | mapped |\n   356|| — | — | JTBD-04 | related | reverse | mapped |\n   357|\n   358|---\n   359|\n   360|## dependencies\n   361|\n   362|### DEP-01: Upstream consumer systems\n   363|- type: external\n   364|- description: at least one upstream system (backend service, reporting pipeline, automation workflow, or AI agent) must integrate with the calculate operation for the service to deliver value | source: project-brief.md §7 Success Criteria\n   365|- owner: not stated in inputs\n   366|- impact_if_unavailable: no consumer integration means the service delivers no business value regardless of correctness | source: project-brief.md §7\n   367|- source: project-brief.md §7 Success Criteria\n   368|- status: pending\n   369|\n   370|### DEP-02: Embedded persistence abstraction for optional history\n   371|- type: technical\n   372|- description: optional history tracking relies on a lightweight local persistence behind an embedded database abstraction, replaceable without changing the API contract | source: project-brief.md §2 History Tracking, §3 Architectural Positioning\n   373|- owner: not stated in inputs\n   374|- impact_if_unavailable: history tracking and history retrieval (FR-06, FR-08) cannot be delivered; calculations themselves are unaffected because persistence must never block calculation (FR-07) | source: project-brief.md §2, §5\n   375|- source: project-brief.md §2, §3, §5\n   376|- status: complete\n   377|\n   378|### inter_feature_dependencies\n   379|- FR-02 (Execute advanced operations) depends_on FR-01 (Execute standard arithmetic operations): advanced operations extend the same calculate operation contract established by standard operations | source: project-brief.md §2; discovery-transcript.md §01:30 | status: complete\n   380|- FR-03 (Produce deterministic, high-precision results) depends_on FR-01 (Execute standard arithmetic operations): determinism applies to the operations FR-01 defines | source: project-brief.md §2, §5 | status: complete\n   381|- FR-04 (Expose a calculate operation via `POST /api/v1/math/calculate`) depends_on FR-01 (Execute standard arithmetic operations): the endpoint exposes the operations FR-01 defines | source: project-brief.md §4 | status: complete\n   382|- FR-05 (Return clear, fail-fast errors for invalid input) depends_on FR-01 (Execute standard arithmetic operations): error handling governs requests against the calculate operation | source: discovery-transcript.md §10:00 | status: complete\n   383|- FR-06 (Record calculation history as optional, auxiliary local persistence) depends_on FR-01 (Execute standard arithmetic operations): history records metadata of calculations performed via the calculate operation | source: project-brief.md §2 | status: complete\n   384|- FR-07 (Calculation must succeed even if history persistence fails) depends_on FR-06 (Record calculation history as optional, auxiliary local persistence): the persistence-failure guarantee is meaningful only when history recording exists | source: project-brief.md §5 Reliability | status: complete\n   385|- FR-08 (Provide history retrieval of recent calculation records) depends_on FR-06 (Record calculation history as optional, auxiliary local persistence): retrieval requires recorded history | source: project-brief.md §4 | status: complete\n   386|- FR-09 (Support extensibility so new operations are added without breaking existing consumers) depends_on FR-01 (Execute standard arithmetic operations): extensibility preserves the contract FR-01 establishes | source: discovery-transcript.md §01:30 | status: complete\n   387|\n   388|---\n   389|\n   390|## risks\n   391|\n   392|### RSK-01: Hidden logic differences remain between systems after the service launches\n   393|- likelihood: medium\n   394|- impact: high\n   395|- mitigation: centralize all calculation logic in the service and enforce deterministic, high-precision computation (FR-03) so all systems receive identical results; validate with deterministic unit test coverage for all operations (KPI-02)\n   396|- source: discovery-transcript.md §13:30 (\"Hidden logic differences between systems\")\n   397|- status: complete\n   398|\n   399|### RSK-02: Teams bypass the service and keep embedding their own calculation logic\n   400|- likelihood: medium\n</file>","isError":false,"durationMs":562}}
