[08:51:05] {"type":"tool_end","payload":{"toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-b7b0912fc1b61543","toolName":"read","result":"<file path=\"C:\\IA Trainings\\CalcService\\context-pack\\system\\execution-protocol.md\" lines=\"1541\" start=\"121\" end=\"240\" returned=\"120\" has_more=\"true\" truncated=\"false\">\n   121|  - §11.6 Open Questions in the sidecar (the reserved `_open_questions` row — only what THIS run raised, `[]` when nothing)\n   122|- §12 — Delegated exploration (read-only subagent fan-out, harness-agnostic, optional)\n   123|  - §12.1 When to delegate (and when not)\n   124|  - §12.2 The return contract (conclusions + `file:line`, never file dumps)\n   125|    - §12.2.1 Anti-patterns — hard stops (no verbatim dumps, no known paths, no duplicate reads)\n   126|  - §12.3 Model selection (prefer the cheap/fast tier)\n   127|  - §12.4 Verify before use (subagent output is input, not ground truth)\n   128|- §13 — Code-location discipline (consult `codebase-map.md` before any discovery scan; code-searching skills only)\n   129|  - §13.1 The rule — consult before scan, not never scan\n   130|  - §13.2 When the map is insufficient — flag the gap\n   131|  - §13.3 Applies to (code-searching skills only)\n   132|  - §13.4 Composition with §12\n   133|  - §12.5 Fallback (no subagent capability → inline exploration)\n   134|  - §12.6 Skill-level enforcement\n   135|- §14 — Parallel fan-out (write-capable subagent fan-out over independent units; harness-agnostic, optional)\n   136|  - §14.1 When to fan out (and when not)\n   137|  - §14.2 Worker contract (incl. §14.2.1 sharded progress)\n   138|  - §14.3 Coordinator contract (shard → dispatch → merge → sidecar last)\n   139|  - §14.4 Verify before use\n   140|  - §14.5 Fallback (no delegation capability → inline unit loop)\n   141|  - §14.6 Skill-level enforcement\n   142|- §15 — Deliverable upload safety (web-shaped deliverables cross a byte-scanning WAF; HTML/SVG/JS-emitting skills only)\n   143|  - §15.1 Recognising the failure (403 + `text/html` = the request never reached the app)\n   144|  - §15.2 Forbidden byte patterns (chained bare `alert(`, SQLi signatures — in code, mock data, or prose)\n   145|  - §15.3 Authoring rules (no native browser dialogs; describe payloads, don't reproduce them)\n   146|  - §15.4 Pre-delivery scan — mandatory gate before the §11 sidecar\n   147|  - §15.5 Optional live-firewall verification\n   148|- §16 — Skill reference file resolution (universal — every skill with a `references/` folder)\n   149|  - §16.1 The rule — resolve relative to the skill's own directory, never search for it\n   150|  - §16.2 Why unscoped search fails here (`.coda/skills/` is gitignored; a gitignore-respecting `glob` returns zero hits even though the file exists)\n   151|  - §16.3 If a bundled reference truly appears missing\n   152|\n   153|---\n   154|\n   155|\n   156|## Section files\n   157|\n   158|Non-universal sections now live as individual files under `execution-protocol/`.\n   159|Read only those your route selects (see the routing rule above); each is readable\n   160|standalone with its original § numbering intact. The **universal** sections (§2,\n   161|§10.5, §11 — required by every skill) remain inlined below.\n   162|\n   163|| § | Location |\n   164||---|----------|\n   165|| §1  Session Identity | `execution-protocol/01-session-identity.md` |\n   166|| §2  `_progress.json` | inline below (universal) |\n   167|| §3  IMPL-STATE | `execution-protocol/03-impl-state.md` |\n   168|| §4  Memory Bank | `execution-protocol/04-memory-bank.md` |\n   169|| §5  FIC recovery | `execution-protocol/05-fic-recovery.md` |\n   170|| §6  Dual-format | `execution-protocol/06-dual-format.md` |\n   171|| §7  REPAIR / §7.3 REBUILD | `execution-protocol/07-repair-mode.md` |\n   172|| §8  Deviations | `execution-protocol/08-deviations.md` |\n   173|| §9  Non-interactive | `execution-protocol/09-non-interactive.md` |\n   174|| §10 Section generation | `execution-protocol/10-section-generation.md` |\n   175|| §10.5 Tool discipline | inline below (universal) |\n   176|| §11 Output sidecar | inline below (universal) |\n   177|| §12 Delegated exploration | `execution-protocol/12-delegated-exploration.md` |\n   178|| §13 Code-location | `execution-protocol/13-code-location.md` |\n   179|| §14 Parallel fan-out | `execution-protocol/14-parallel-fanout.md` |\n   180|| §15 Deliverable upload safety | `execution-protocol/15-deliverable-upload-safety.md` |\n   181|| §16 Skill reference file resolution | inline below (universal) |\n   182|\n   183|A skill's route (e.g. `§2 → §10 → §10.5 → §11`) resolves each §N via this table:\n   184|universal sections are already here; the rest are one file-read away. Existing\n   185|\"see execution-protocol.md §N\" citations in the 123 skills keep working through\n   186|this one redirect hop — no skill edits required.\n   187|\n   188|---\n   189|\n   190|## 2. _progress.json — Early Signal Write\n   191|\n   192|**Purpose:** Prevents the orchestrator coordinator from sending SIGINT to a running skill.\n   193|\n   194|Write this as the **FIRST file action** in any skill execution (before creating any other outputs):\n   195|\n   196|```json\n   197|{\n   198|  \"skill\": \"{skill-name}\",\n   199|  \"session_id\": \"{SESSION_ID}\",\n   200|  \"status\": \"RUNNING\",\n   201|  \"started_at\": \"{ISO-8601 UTC, e.g. 2026-08-15T13:21:37Z}\",\n   202|  \"completed_at\": null,\n   203|  \"total\": 0,\n   204|  \"completed\": 0,\n   205|  \"items\": []\n   206|}\n   207|```\n   208|\n   209|Write to: `{output_folder}/_progress.json`\n   210|\n   211|**Before that write, check the path budget — §2.c.** A successful `_progress.json`\n   212|write is not evidence the output folder can hold your actual artifact.\n   213|\n   214|**Update cadence:**\n   215|- On FIC trigger (Section 5): `\"status\": \"COMPACTION_NEEDED\"`\n   216|- On completion: `\"status\": \"COMPLETED\"`, `\"completed_at\": \"{ISO-8601 UTC}\"`\n   217|- On failure: `\"status\": \"FAILED\"`, `\"completed_at\": \"{ISO-8601 UTC}\"`\n   218|\n   219|**UTC, not local time with a `Z` on it.** Read the real clock and convert. A local timestamp\n   220|labelled `Z` reads as a lie about when the work ran — Stepwise stamps its own state in true UTC,\n   221|so the two disagree by your offset and every reviewer sees fabricated evidence where there was\n   222|only a wrong clock. If you cannot get UTC, omit the field rather than guess.\n   223|\n   224|**Status vocabulary — closed set.** Every value carries distinct semantics; the set is\n   225|closed so a monitor can classify any tracker without knowing which skill wrote it.\n   226|\n   227|| status | meaning | live? |\n   228||---|---|---|\n   229|| `RUNNING` | step is executing | yes |\n   230|| `PARTIAL` | chunked step finished its chunk with work remaining; a later invocation resumes | yes |\n   231|| `COMPACTION_NEEDED` | FIC trigger (§5); awaiting compaction | yes |\n   232|| `COMPLETED` | all declared work done | no — terminal |\n   233|| `FAILED` | step could not complete | no — terminal |\n   234|| `SCOPE_REFUSED` | input exceeded the skill's scope envelope; step declined and recommended decomposition rather than producing a degraded artifact | no — terminal |\n   235|\n   236|**Never invent a synonym for a value already in the table** — `IN_PROGRESS`, `STARTED`,\n   237|`OK`, `DONE` and similar are prohibited precisely because they mean `RUNNING` or\n   238|`COMPLETED` while reading as unrecognized to a monitor keying on the set above.\n   239|\n   240|Adding a genuinely new state (distinct semantics, not a synonym) means adding a row to\n</file>","isError":false,"durationMs":16}}
