[08:51:10] {"type":"tool_start","payload":{"toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-94a3ceb6552acb38","toolName":"read","args":{"path":"C:\\IA Trainings\\CalcService\\context-pack\\system\\execution-protocol.md","offset":660,"limit":260}}}
[08:51:10] {"type":"tool_end","payload":{"toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-94a3ceb6552acb38","toolName":"read","result":"<file path=\"C:\\IA Trainings\\CalcService\\context-pack\\system\\execution-protocol.md\" lines=\"1541\" start=\"660\" end=\"919\" returned=\"260\" has_more=\"true\" truncated=\"false\">\n   660|   recognise the line, and it will report the hash as missing rather than wrong.\n   661|2. Run this, substituting your artifact's path:\n   662|\n   663|   ```bash\n   664|   python3 - \"$SPEC_FILE\" <<'EOF'\n   665|   import hashlib, re, sys\n   666|   path = sys.argv[1]\n   667|   text = open(path, encoding='utf-8').read()\n   668|   m = re.search(r'^content_hash:\\s*([0-9a-f]{64})\\s*\\n?', text, re.M)\n   669|   if not m:\n   670|       sys.exit('content_hash line missing or not 64 hex characters')\n   671|   canonical = text[:m.start()] + text[m.end():]\n   672|   digest = hashlib.sha256(canonical.encode('utf-8')).hexdigest()\n   673|   open(path, 'w', encoding='utf-8').write(\n   674|       text[:m.start()] + f'content_hash: {digest}\\n' + text[m.end():]\n   675|   )\n   676|   print(digest)\n   677|   EOF\n   678|   ```\n   679|\n   680|3. The digest it prints is the value now in the file. Report that value if your\n   681|   sidecar or audit asks for it — do not retype it from memory.\n   682|\n   683|Removing the whole `content_hash:` line yields the same canonical text whatever\n   684|value sits on it, which is what makes this converge in one pass: the verifier\n   685|strips the line exactly as this does.\n   686|\n   687|**This is the one sanctioned exception to the `python3`-mutation ban above.**\n   688|That ban exists to stop bulk section rewrites that bypass `Edit`'s prior-content\n   689|validation. This script rewrites a single header line whose correct value cannot\n   690|be known before the file is final, it runs once after every section is written,\n   691|and it is the only way the field can be right.\n   692|\n   693|---\n   694|\n   695|#### 10.5.5 Tool-Denial Adaptation (mandatory) — a denial is terminal for that exact command\n   696|\n   697|**A tool/command-policy denial (`decision: deny`, an approval-required refusal, or\n   698|equivalent) is a terminal signal for the exact call that was denied — never a\n   699|transient failure to retry as-is.** The environment is telling you, deterministically,\n   700|that this literal command will not be allowed to run; issuing it again produces the\n   701|identical denial, not a different outcome.\n   702|\n   703|Field evidence (2026-09-12, FairShare `local-runtime-validation`, `smoke-probing`\n   704|step): an HTTP health probe (`curl -s http://localhost:3000/health/ready`) was denied\n   705|by the project's command policy, which returned `suggestedAlternatives:\n   706|[\"Bash(pnpm:*)\",\"Bash(bun:*)\",\"Bash(bun run test:*)\"]` in the same response. The agent\n   707|reissued the byte-identical denied command **266 times** until it hit `max_steps` —\n   708|**31.8M tokens** for zero forward progress, and the step ended `exec_failed` with no\n   709|outputs at all. The alternatives were returned and never read, let alone acted on.\n   710|\n   711|The identical shape then recurred twice more in the same project, in two different\n   712|skills, on two different commands: `bootstrapping-runtime-environment` issued\n   713|`bun run prisma:generate` (124+ identical denials) and, separately,\n   714|`launching-app` issued `bun run build` (201 identical denials, manually\n   715|interrupted) — both denied for the same reason (\"approval is required, but no\n   716|operator is present\"), both returning the same `suggestedAlternatives`, neither\n   717|ever read. This is not three unrelated incidents; it is one recurring shape —\n   718|**a package.json script invoked via `<manager> run <script>`** — that any skill\n   719|shelling out in an npm-ecosystem project can hit, not a defect local to one skill.\n   720|\n   721|**The rule:**\n   722|\n   723|1. On any denial, do not reissue the same command (same binary, same arguments) a\n   724|   second time. One denial is information; a second identical one is not — it is\n   725|   pure waste, since the policy has not changed between your two calls.\n   726|2. Read the denial payload for `suggestedAlternatives` (or an equivalent hint) and\n   727|   for the project's command policy generally. If an allowed alternative can perform\n   728|   the SAME operation — a different program, a different invocation shape, a\n   729|   language runtime already on the allowlist — rewrite the call using it and\n   730|   continue the SAME plan; only the transport/mechanism changes, not the goal.\n   731|3. If no allowed alternative can perform the operation at all, that is a hard\n   732|   blocker, not a retry target: stop after that one adaptation attempt, record the\n   733|   blocker explicitly (open_questions / audit / tracker — never a silent\n   734|   zero-finding PASS), and signal failure (`stepwise session exec-fail` or the\n   735|   skill's equivalent) naming the missing capability. Do not spend the remaining\n   736|   step budget on further attempts of the same denied operation.\n   737|\n   738|**The concrete recipe for the recurring `<manager> run <script>` shape** (this is\n   739|the single most common case rule 2 will need in practice): `<manager> run\n   740|<script-name>` asks the sandbox to resolve and execute an ARBITRARY named script\n   741|from `package.json` — an approval policy that pre-authorizes a known binary prefix\n   742|(`bun`, `bunx`, `pnpm`, `npx`) cannot pre-authorize an arbitrary script name, and\n   743|denies it on sight, every time, identically. Before running any package.json\n   744|script, or immediately on its first denial:\n   745|\n   746|1. Read the script's definition from `package.json`'s `scripts` block.\n   747|2. If it is a SINGLE binary invocation (e.g. `\"build\": \"tsc -p tsconfig.json\"`,\n   748|   `\"prisma:generate\": \"prisma generate\"`) — call that binary directly through the\n   749|   allowlisted runner for the detected package manager: `bunx <binary> <args>` /\n   750|   `pnpm exec <binary> <args>` / `npx <binary> <args>` / `yarn dlx <binary> <args>`.\n   751|   This is not a guess or a workaround; it produces the identical program run,\n   752|   through a form the policy can actually authorize.\n   753|3. If it is COMPOUND (`&&`, `;`, `|`, multiple programs) there is no single-binary\n   754|   equivalent — do not invent one. This is a hard blocker per rule 3 above: record\n   755|   that the project's command policy has no path to run this specific script, and\n   756|   name the exact script. (A project can pre-approve a specific compound script,\n   757|   e.g. `Bash(bun run test:*)` in the evidence above — that is a policy decision\n   758|   for the project to make, not something a skill can route around.)\n   759|\n   760|**Applies to:** every skill that can shell out or call a tool subject to approval or\n   761|command-policy gating — this is universal, not specific to one tool or one skill.\n   762|`api-smoke-probe`, `bootstrapping-runtime-environment`, and `launching-app` each\n   763|carry the incident's own Known Failure Modes entry as concrete instances that\n   764|produced or reinforced this rule.\n   765|\n   766|---\n   767|\n   768|## 11. Harness Output Sidecar\n   769|\n   770|**Purpose.** The Stepwise harness reads each step's declared outputs from a sidecar JSON file written at a path the harness controls. If this file is missing, the orchestrator records `outputs: {}` in `state.json`, the UI's artifact panel renders empty, and downstream steps cannot pick up output values via propagation. **Artifacts on disk are not enough** — the sidecar is the framework contract between the skill and the orchestrator.\n   771|\n   772|**Applicability.** Any skill that may be invoked by Stepwise. Standalone invocations (no orchestrator) do not need the sidecar — see the trigger gate below.\n   773|\n   774|**When this section applies.** The input prompt contains a `## Run metadata` block with an `output_file = '<absolute path>'` line. If that block is absent (e.g. standalone invocation outside Stepwise), skip the sidecar write entirely.\n   775|\n   776|---\n   777|\n   778|### 11.1 Mechanism\n   779|\n   780|```\n   781|1. Re-read the prompt's `## Run metadata` block. Copy the path on the\n   782|   `output_file = '...'` line VERBATIM. Do NOT reconstruct it from runId,\n   783|   session name, project name, or any other source — the path the harness\n   784|   wrote there is the path the harness expects to read from.\n   785|\n   786|2. Re-read the prompt's `## Output parameters` table. For EVERY row in that\n   787|   table, include a key in the JSON object:\n   788|     - key   = the parameter name from the table\n   789|     - value = the resolved value the skill produced for that output\n   790|               (typically the path to the artifact folder or file)\n   791|\n   792|3. Write that JSON object to the `output_file` path. This is the FINAL file\n   793|   write of the run — strict ordering (see Section 11.4):\n   794|     - AFTER any skill-defined output verification (e.g. file-existence checks).\n   795|     - AFTER Memory Bank session-end writes (Section 4).\n   796|     - AFTER `_progress.json` flipped to `status: COMPLETED` (Section 2 LAST ACTION).\n   797|\n   798|4. Do NOT emit any tool call after the sidecar write. The next event in the\n   799|   transcript should be the `final_response` summary.\n   800|```\n   801|\n   802|---\n   803|\n   804|### 11.1.1 Path Correctness Rule (mandatory)\n   805|\n   806|**First, resolving the path you were given — before any write.** A path parameter can arrive\n   807|as a bare fragment (`prd`, `test-cases`, `domain-boundaries`) rather than a rooted path,\n   808|because the capability's `value_template` passed the operator's value through without\n   809|composing it. A fragment is **relative to this run's output directory**, never to your\n   810|working directory:\n   811|\n   812|```\n   813|GIVEN a path-typed input parameter P:\n   814|  IF P starts with \"/\" or \"./\" or \"../\"  -> use P as-is (already rooted)\n   815|  ELSE                                   -> P = <this run's output directory> + \"/\" + P\n   816|```\n   817|\n   818|Your run's output directory is normally the prompt's \"All the generated artifacts should be\n   819|placed in the output directory: ...\" line, or the `output_folder` parameter. `mkdir`-ing a\n   820|bare fragment creates a stray folder at the repository root, outside `artifacts/outputs/`,\n   821|where no downstream step or gate will look for it.\n   822|\n   823|**If the prompt gives you no rooted anchor, do not guess.** A capability whose instruction\n   824|renders that line from the same uncomposed parameter — `output directory: prd` — and which\n   825|omits `output_folder` from its parameter table has left you nothing to resolve against.\n   826|Silently falling back to the working directory is what produces the stray folder. Treat it as\n   827|a capability defect: proceed with the value you were given, and raise it as a blocking open\n   828|question naming the parameter and what you assumed, so the wiring gets fixed rather than the\n   829|symptom recurring every run.\n   830|\n   831|**Symptom.** Your outputs land next to the project's top-level folders instead of under\n   832|`artifacts/outputs/<capability>/`. Observed 2026-09-06 (`pli-20260906-qxlefw`): a playlist set\n   833|`prd_output_path: \"prd\"` — consistent with every other capability in that playlist, which\n   834|compose fragments in their `value_template` — the producing capability passed it through\n   835|unchanged, and the step created `<project>/prd/` while the prompt's stated output directory\n   836|was `<project>/artifacts/outputs/product-definition`. The verification gate then failed\n   837|against the folder nobody wrote to.\n   838|\n   839|Report the composed path per the rule below; the parameter you were handed is the input, not\n   840|the answer.\n   841|\n   842|---\n   843|\n   844|The value reported for any output **path** parameter MUST be the **actual on-disk path the skill wrote\n   845|to** — never a re-derived input parameter, never a fresh `value_template` substitution, and never a\n   846|scope-/sibling-adjusted path that was NOT the one actually used for the write.\n   847|\n   848|```\n   849|BEFORE writing the sidecar, for every path-typed output parameter:\n   850|  1. Confirm the artifact exists at the path you are about to report\n   851|     (e.g. `ls <PATH>/<EXPECTED-FILE>*` or a stat/existence check).\n   852|  2. If the path the skill actually wrote to differs from the input parameter\n   853|     for ANY reason — sibling-folder rule, scope adjustment, feature_id stripping,\n   854|     SESSION_ID derivation — report the ACTUAL written path, NOT the parameter.\n   855|  3. Reporting a parameter you did not write to is the documented cause of the\n   856|     duplicate-artifact-on-REPAIR defect: a later REPAIR run is handed the wrong\n   857|     folder, finds no prior artifact, and silently rebuilds a duplicate. (See §7.)\n   858|```\n   859|\n   860|**Worked example (sibling rule).** A planning step receives `planning_output_path =\n   861|/base/code-development/code-task-planning` but, because `feature_id` was empty and the upstream research\n   862|was scoped, Step 1 actually wrote to `/base/code-development/TICKET-001/code-task-planning/`. The sidecar\n   863|MUST report the latter (the path that holds the files), never the flat input parameter.\n   864|\n   865|> **Note for skill authors:** \"report the parameter VERBATIM\" guidance (used by some skills to avoid\n   866|> pre-resolution double-prefixing) means *do not re-prepend project/scope segments yourself* — it does\n   867|> NOT license reporting a path you did not write to. When your own logic diverges from the parameter,\n   868|> the actual written path always wins.\n   869|\n   870|---\n   871|\n   872|### 11.1.2 Verdict Fidelity Rule (mandatory)\n   873|\n   874|§11.1.1 governs path-typed outputs. This rule governs **closed-set outputs** — any output parameter\n   875|that declares an enumerated `values:` array, which in practice means every gate / verdict-gated-transition\n   876|(VGT) verdict the orchestrator routes on.\n   877|\n   878|The value reported for such a parameter MUST be **one of the declared tokens, copied character-for-character**.\n   879|\n   880|```\n   881|BEFORE writing the sidecar, for every output parameter with a declared `values:` set:\n   882|  1. Re-read the allowed tokens from the prompt's `## Output parameters` table\n   883|     (or, when the table omits them, from this skill's own verdict definition).\n   884|  2. Emit one of those tokens EXACTLY: same spelling, same case, same underscores.\n   885|     No synonym, no title-casing, no pluralisation, no added qualifier, no\n   886|     translation, no \"PASSED (with notes)\".\n   887|  3. Confirm the emitted token is a member of the declared set — a literal string\n   888|     comparison, not \"close enough\". If your computed verdict does not map onto a\n   889|     declared token, that is a bug in your mapping: pick the correct declared token,\n   890|     do not invent one.\n   891|  4. Confirm the token AGREES with the artifact you just wrote. When the report /\n   892|     spec carries the verdict in its own front-matter or summary, the two MUST be\n   893|     identical. A report that says PASSED and a sidecar that says something else is\n   894|     a self-contradiction, and the sidecar is what the orchestrator acts on.\n   895|```\n   896|\n   897|**Why this is not cosmetic.** The orchestrator matches the reported token against the step's\n   898|`gate_policy` accept/reject lists. A token in neither list is **unroutable**, so the engine\n   899|**fails closed and treats it as a reject** — the only safe default, since silently passing an\n   900|unrecognised verdict through a correctness gate would be worse. The consequence is a spurious\n   901|retry loop: the upstream producer step is re-run, every step between it and the gate is reset and\n   902|re-run with it, and the retry budget is consumed. When the budget is exhausted the run escalates\n   903|to a human gate carrying a rejection that never happened.\n   904|\n   905|**Worked example (observed).** A plan-verification gate declared\n   906|`values: [PASSED, PASSED_WITH_FINDINGS, FAILED]`. The skill wrote a report whose front-matter read\n   907|`verification_verdict: PASSED`, `weighted_total: 100`, `failed_blocker_count: 0` — a clean pass — but\n   908|emitted `\"verification_verdict\": \"APPROVED\"` in the sidecar. `APPROVED` is not in the set, so the gate\n   909|failed closed, rejected, and re-ran planning plus the two steps between. A perfect-scoring plan was\n   910|re-planned because the sidecar used a synonym.\n   911|\n   912|**Robustness note.** This write is the last action of a run, often after a long generation, and may be\n   913|performed under a degraded executor — a fallback model, a smaller tier after a quota or rate-limit\n   914|failure, or a resumed session. Those are exactly the conditions under which a token gets paraphrased.\n   915|Treat the closed-set comparison in step 3 as a real check to perform at write time, not a stylistic\n   916|preference — it is cheap, and it is the only thing standing between a correct verdict and a wasted lap.\n   917|\n   918|---\n   919|\n</file>","isError":false,"durationMs":7}}
