[08:51:12] {"type":"tool_end","payload":{"toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-b17a957430c5f5d9","toolName":"read","result":"<file path=\"C:\\IA Trainings\\CalcService\\context-pack\\system\\execution-protocol.md\" lines=\"1541\" start=\"920\" end=\"1159\" returned=\"240\" has_more=\"true\" truncated=\"false\">\n   920|### 11.2 Example\n   921|\n   922|For a skill that declares a single output parameter named `foo_output_path`:\n   923|\n   924|```json\n   925|{ \"foo_output_path\": \"/abs/path/to/artifacts/outputs/<capability>/<step>\" }\n   926|```\n   927|\n   928|For a skill with multiple output parameters, include every row from the `## Output parameters` table — one key per row. The skill-specific parameter names live in each SKILL.md (Section 11.5); this section does not enumerate them.\n   929|\n   930|---\n   931|\n   932|### 11.3 Violation\n   933|\n   934|Ending a Stepwise run with a `final_response` summary that lists the generated artifacts but no sidecar write breaks output propagation. The harness MAY have a `value_template` fallback for specific outputs, but that fallback is not guaranteed for every skill / every output. Treat the sidecar write as **non-optional** whenever the `## Run metadata` block is present in the prompt — do not rely on the fallback.\n   935|\n   936|Skills whose outputs lack a `value_template` fallback fail **silently** when the sidecar is missing: downstream steps see `outputs: {}` and propagate empty values; no error surfaces in the run. This is the failure mode this section prevents.\n   937|\n   938|---\n   939|\n   940|### 11.4 Composition with Section 2 LAST ACTION\n   941|\n   942|The sidecar write extends Section 2's LAST ACTION; it does NOT replace it. Strict ordering for any Stepwise-invoked skill:\n   943|\n   944|1. All skill-specific outputs written and verified.\n   945|2. Memory Bank writes (Section 4 — `active-context.md`, `progress.md`).\n   946|3. `_progress.json` flipped to `status: COMPLETED` (Section 2 LAST ACTION).\n   947|4. **Sidecar write** (this section) — final file write of the run.\n   948|5. `final_response` summary — no further tool calls.\n   949|\n   950|If the skill is invoked standalone (no `## Run metadata` block), step 4 is skipped. Steps 1–3 and 5 still apply.\n   951|\n   952|---\n   953|\n   954|### 11.5 Skill-Level Enforcement\n   955|\n   956|Section 11 cannot enumerate output parameter names — those are declared per-skill in the capability YAML's `outputs` block and surface in each prompt's `## Output parameters` table. Each SKILL.md MUST include a short inline callout naming its output parameters, so the agent knows which keys to put in the sidecar JSON without parsing the YAML.\n   957|\n   958|Recommended SKILL.md callout shape:\n   959|\n   960|> **Step N: Emit harness outputs sidecar — apply execution-protocol.md Section 11.**\n   961|>\n   962|> **Output parameters this skill produces** (one key per row of the prompt's `## Output parameters` table):\n   963|>\n   964|> - `<param_name_1>`: <one-line description of the resolved value, e.g. \"the resolved output folder path written in earlier steps\">\n   965|> - `<param_name_2>`: ...\n   966|>\n   967|> Example sidecar contents (illustrative):\n   968|>\n   969|> ```json\n   970|> { \"<param_name_1>\": \"/abs/path/...\", \"<param_name_2>\": \"...\" }\n   971|> ```\n   972|>\n   973|> **The sidecar ALSO carries the reserved `_open_questions` key — see §11.6.** It is not one\n   974|> of this skill's declared outputs — Stepwise itself renders it as a required row of the\n   975|> `## Output parameters` table — so it is not listed above; that is why the example shows\n   976|> only declared keys. Write it in every sidecar: every open question THIS run raised, `[]`\n   977|> when it raised nothing. Name the registries this skill writes (e.g. \"the standard\n   978|> `pending_inputs` / `traceability_gaps` / `assumptions_to_validate` tables\") and stop\n   979|> there — §11.6 owns the field set and the mapping.\n   980|\n   981|The callout is intentionally short. Trigger gate, mechanism, ordering, and violation rule all live in Section 11 — the SKILL.md only names the keys.\n   982|\n   983|**A worked example outranks a rule the reader has to recall.** A callout whose example shows\n   984|only declared parameters is read as the complete sidecar contract for that skill, and it will\n   985|beat §11.6 at the moment of writing — observed 2026-09-05, where a skill with a\n   986|fourteen-row open-questions registry emitted a one-key sidecar and the orchestrator had to\n   987|scrape the artifact instead. That is why the paragraph above belongs in the callout rather\n   988|than being left implicit.\n   989|\n   990|---\n   991|\n   992|\n   993|### 11.6 Open Questions in the sidecar (universal)\n   994|\n   995|**Trigger.** The prompt has a `## Run metadata` block (§11 trigger gate). That is the only condition: the key is written on EVERY sidecar, whether or not this run raised a question.\n   996|\n   997|**Rule.** Write the reserved `_open_questions` key in the same sidecar JSON as your declared outputs — ALWAYS. If this run wrote an `## Open Questions` / `## open_questions` section (any heading level, any spelling) into an artifact, mirror that section into it; if this run raised nothing, write `[]`. This is not extra analysis: you already decided what the open questions are and wrote them down. This emits the same list in a form the orchestrator can act on, and the empty array is how you tell it, unambiguously, that there was nothing to track.\n   998|\n   999|**Only what THIS run raised — never what the prompt handed you, and never what the project has already settled.** If a question appears in your prompt's `## OPEN QUESTIONS`, `## DECISIONS` or `## PROJECT DECISIONS` block, it is already tracked. The same holds for a decision you meet anywhere else you read — a value tagged `[OQ-xxxxxxxx]` in an upstream artifact, a row of `context-pack/decisions.md`, or the count in the `## PROJECT DECISIONS` pointer sentence (\"N further settled decisions are not repeated here\"): the block carries only what nothing else in your inputs holds yet, so most settled decisions reach you through the documents, not the block. A settled decision is not a question. Do not put it in your artifact's open-questions section, and do not declare it in `_open_questions` — not under your own numbering, not re-worded in your own voice, not \"for completeness\", and not as an assumption that reads \"…, per settled decision OQ-xxxxxxxx\" (observed 2026-09-09, `backlog-story-generation`: four such rows, each re-asking a decision it had already applied, each answered with the very value it cited). Where your work depends on one, cite its id in the sentence that proceeds on it:\n  1000|\n  1001|```\n  1002|- EPIC-01 asume el claim `smart.legajo` como fuente de identidad del analista (OQ-bd39279d).\n  1003|```\n  1004|\n  1005|`OQ-xxxxxxxx` (eight hex characters) is Stepwise's ledger id, and citing it is how an\n  1006|artifact shows which decision or open question a sentence rests on. It is a cross-reference,\n  1007|not one of the artifact's own ids: a verifier that checks \"every id uses an allowed prefix\"\n  1008|must treat `OQ-[0-9a-f]{8}` as always allowed, or it fails the exact behaviour this\n  1009|section requires (observed 2026-09-08: CHK-008 on a PRD flagged `OQ-3551076d` as a\n  1010|disallowed id and rejected a correctly cited document).\n  1011|\n  1012|**Why this is a hard rule and not a preference.** Identity is a hash of the question text, so a re-worded copy does not match the original — it opens a SECOND row that no automatic merge can close, and the operator has to recognise and fold it by hand. Measured on a live run: a step was handed 33 inherited questions and 17 decisions in its prompt, re-listed them in its own artifact under its own numbering, mirrored all of it into `_open_questions`, and turned 74 questions into 124. Thirty-seven were the same question asked twice.\n  1013|\n  1014|This applies to the SIDECAR as much as to the artifact section. The earlier form of this rule addressed only the prose, on the assumption that the sidecar mirrors the prose — so a skill that writes the inherited questions into its section complies with the mirror rule and breaks the ledger anyway. Both halves are the same rule: **the open-questions registry of an artifact, and its sidecar, describe what this step discovered.**\n  1015|\n  1016|`_open_questions` is a RESERVED row. Stepwise itself renders it into the `## Output parameters` table of every step prompt, marked required; it is never one of the skill's declared outputs, and Stepwise strips it before output resolution, so writing it never breaks propagation. An absent key is a contract violation that costs one repair turn — write `[]` rather than leaving it out. Stepwise validates entries at the boundary: a `question` without `assumption_if_unanswered` is accepted but flagged \"needs a basis\" and can never be bulk-accepted; an `answer_shape: enum` without `options` is downgraded to free text.\n  1017|\n  1018|```json\n  1019|{\n  1020|  \"prd_output_path\": \"/abs/path/to/artifacts/outputs/product-definition/prd\",\n  1021|  \"_open_questions\": [\n  1022|    {\n  1023|      \"id\": \"PI-10\",\n  1024|      \"question\": \"Which inventory slice does the pilot run on?\",\n  1025|      \"subject\": \"scope\",\n  1026|      \"why_it_matters\": \"Every downstream sizing estimate assumes one slice\",\n  1027|      \"blocking\": true,\n  1028|      \"assumption_if_unanswered\": \"one DTC channel with real sell-out visibility\",\n  1029|      \"kind\": \"question\",\n  1030|      \"answer_shape\": \"enum\",\n  1031|      \"options\": [\n  1032|        { \"id\": \"dtc\", \"label\": \"One DTC channel with real sell-out visibility\" },\n  1033|        { \"id\": \"wholesale\", \"label\": \"The wholesale slice\" },\n  1034|        { \"id\": \"all\", \"label\": \"Every channel\" }\n  1035|      ],\n  1036|      \"audience\": \"client\",\n  1037|      \"artifact_ref\": \"artifacts/outputs/product-definition/PRD-SPEC-....md#open-questions\",\n  1038|      \"traces_to\": [\"EPIC-02\", \"FR-14\"]\n  1039|    }\n  1040|  ]\n  1041|}\n  1042|```\n  1043|\n  1044|When this run raised nothing:\n  1045|\n  1046|```json\n  1047|{ \"prd_output_path\": \"/abs/path/to/artifacts/outputs/product-definition/prd\", \"_open_questions\": [] }\n  1048|```\n  1049|\n  1050|**Fields.** `question`, `audience`, `artifact_ref`, `subject` and — on a `question` — `assumption_if_unanswered` are required. Emit every other field you actually have; omit what you do not. Never invent one — Zero-Invention applies here exactly as it does to the artifact.\n  1051|\n  1052|`artifact_ref` is required because the trigger already told you the answer: you are mirroring a section you just wrote INTO an artifact, so you know which artifact it is. There is nothing to look up and nothing to invent.\n  1053|\n  1054|- `id` — your own local id (`PI-10`, `OQ-03`) if the section has one.\n  1055|- `kind` — `question` (default) or `assumption`. See the note below; getting this right decides which verbs the operator is offered. **Every row of an `assumptions_to_validate` table is an `assumption` entry**, whether or not it carries a fallback and whether or not it is blocking: an assumption with a fallback is still a choice the work made on the operator's behalf, and one that stays only in the artifact is a decision nobody was offered. Observed 2026-09-08 (`researching-code-design`): the backend runtime was chosen in an `ASM-01` row, the sidecar said `[]`, and nothing reached the ledger — implementation would have proceeded on Node.js because one table was read as \"not questions\".\n  1056|- `subject` — **REQUIRED.** What the question is ABOUT (`scope`, `compliance`, `personas`, `observability`). It turns a 60-row inbox into a dozen themes — and it is also how a settled decision finds its way into the context pack: Stepwise routes a decision to the topic file whose `when_to_read` its subject overlaps. A decision with no subject can never be routed, so it is re-offered on every reconcile forever and never folded into the pack. Measured on one project: 30 of 126 decisions carried no subject and were permanently unharvestable, from four steps that emitted everything else correctly. Use the noun the registry row is already about; do not invent a taxonomy.\n  1057|- `why_it_matters` — the consequence of leaving it open.\n  1058|- `blocking` — `true` ONLY if downstream work genuinely cannot proceed. See the warning below.\n  1059|- `assumption_if_unanswered` — **REQUIRED on a `question`.** What you actually proceeded on. See **Say what you proceeded on** below. (Omit it on an `assumption`: the claim IS what the work proceeded on.)\n  1060|- `audience` — **REQUIRED.** Who OWNS the answer: `client`, `architect`, `product_owner`, `internal`. See **Every question names an owner** below.\n  1061|- `answer_shape` — `free_text` (default), `enum`, `path`, `number`, `boolean`. Prefer `enum` with 2–5 `options` whenever the answer is a choice: it gives the operator one-click buttons instead of a text box.\n  1062|- `options` — required when `answer_shape` is `enum`: an array of `{ \"id\", \"label\" }` objects (or plain strings). Stepwise downgrades an `enum` without options to free text, so an enum with no options buys nothing.\n  1063|\n  1064|  **Options carry the trade-off, not just the choice.** A label naming only the option (\"Standard\" / \"High-Performance\") makes the operator go read the artifact to find out what either one costs; a label naming the concrete consequence lets them decide from the button alone. Compare \"Standard\" against \"Standard (< 2s response, lower infra cost)\" — same choice, only one is answerable without leaving the inbox. Two further rules apply to every `enum` row: options MUST be mutually exclusive (an operator who could reasonably pick two is being offered one question disguised as two) and MUST cover the reasonable range of answers a downstream artifact would actually be built differently against — not padded with an option nobody would choose to reach the 2–5 count.\n  1065|- `artifact_ref` — **REQUIRED. Project-relative** path (optionally `#anchor`) of the artifact that raised it, with `/` separators — `artifacts/outputs/product-definition/PRD-SPEC-20260812.md#open-questions`. NOT an absolute path: the ledger is committed to the project repo and opened on other people's machines, and an absolute path from yours is unusable on theirs. This field is what the operator's \"Edit the artifact\" button resolves, and on one measured project 997 references named a machine nobody else had (Stepwise issue #1047). Same rule the harness applies to every path it persists.\n  1066|\n  1067|  **Omitting it is not a smaller sidecar, it is an unrepairable question.** The orchestrator computes which documents an answer invalidated from these refs; a question with none contributes no document, so when the operator answers it there is nothing to point a repair at and no \"Edit the artifact\" to offer. Measured on one run: `prd-creation` emitted 75 refs and every one of its questions was repairable, while `backlog-epic-generation` (58) and `discovering-bounded-contexts` (13) emitted none at all — 71 questions that could be asked and answered but never applied. Emit it on every row.\n  1068|- `traces_to` — ids the question affects (`EPIC-02`, `FR-14`, `BC-09`).\n  1069|\n  1070|**Enumerate the registries from YOUR artifact, never from a remembered list.** Agent-native\n  1071|spec skills put several tables under `open_questions`, and the set differs per skill:\n  1072|`pending_inputs`, `coverage_gaps`, `alignment_gaps`, `traceability_gaps`, `missing_inputs`,\n  1073|`assumptions_to_validate`. Before\n  1074|building the sidecar, **read the `###` headings under your own `open_questions` section and\n  1075|mirror every row of every one of them** (except `summary`, which is counts, not questions).\n  1076|A registry you did not mirror is a partial list, and §11.6's completeness rule treats a\n  1077|partial list as \"the rest were resolved\".\n  1078|\n  1079|Observed 2026-09-06 (`backlog-epic-generation`, `pli-20260905-1jcnazb`): a skill was told its\n  1080|registries were `pending_inputs` / `traceability_gaps` / `assumptions_to_validate`, mirrored\n  1081|exactly those, and silently dropped `AG-01` from the `alignment_gaps` table it also had. The\n  1082|orchestrator recovered it by scraping the markdown, where it landed as a row that cannot hold\n  1083|a step. Naming a list is a hint; the artifact on disk is the authority.\n  1084|\n  1085|**Carried-forward registries are retired** (`prd_gaps_carried_forward`,\n  1086|`upstream_gaps_carried_forward` and any `*_gaps_carried_forward` table). The prompt's\n  1087|`## OPEN QUESTIONS` / `## PROJECT DECISIONS` blocks ARE the carry-forward: Stepwise delivers\n  1088|every inherited question and every settled decision there, with its id. Never rebuild one in\n  1089|the artifact and never mirror one into the sidecar; where your work relies on an inherited\n  1090|item, cite its id in the body instead.\n  1091|\n  1092|The columns below are the shape used by `pending_inputs` and its close relatives. **Do not\n  1093|assume any other registry has them** — gap tables frequently do not, and\n  1094|a missing column is never a licence to emit `null`. Map what your table actually has:\n  1095|\n  1096|| sidecar field | registry column |\n  1097||---|---|\n  1098|| `id` | `id` (`PI-XX`, `TG-XX`, `ASM-XX`) |\n  1099|| `question` | `question` on PI/TG rows; the `assumption` statement on ASM rows |\n  1100|| `kind` | `question` for `pending_inputs` and `traceability_gaps`; `assumption` for `assumptions_to_validate` |\n  1101|| `subject` | `section` on PI rows; `traceability` for TG rows; for ASM rows the section the assumption belongs to |\n  1102|| `why_it_matters` | the CONSEQUENCE, in a sentence — what breaks or degrades, named against the `field` / `item_id` it holds up; `impact_if_wrong` on ASM rows. **The bare impact level is not an answer:** `HIGH` is a rejected value, `HIGH — FR-05 cannot be enabled in production until the window is fixed` is the field. Restate the row; do not invent a new consequence |\n  1103|| `blocking` | **re-decided, never copied** — see below. The registry column and this field mean different things |\n  1104|| `assumption_if_unanswered` | `fallback_behavior`, subject to the BLOCKED rule below. Omit on `kind: assumption` |\n  1105|| `traces_to` | `affected_ids` |\n  1106|| `artifact_ref` | the artifact's project-relative path + `#open-questions` |\n  1107|| `audience` | not a column — decide it per row, see **Every question names an owner** |\n  1108|| `inherited_from` | on a repair/iterate lap, the ids from the prompt's `## OPEN QUESTIONS` / `## DECISIONS` / `## PROJECT DECISIONS` blocks this row restates |\n  1109|\n  1110|**The `BLOCKED` collision.** These templates conventionally set\n  1111|`fallback_behavior: BLOCKED` on every `blocking: yes` row, and the rule below forbids refusal\n  1112|tokens in `assumption_if_unanswered`. So where `fallback_behavior` is `BLOCKED`, do NOT copy\n  1113|it — and do NOT reach for a generic stand-in either. Name the narrowest thing THIS artifact\n  1114|actually did about THIS item, in terms of its own ids:\n  1115|\n  1116|> `FR-01 is emitted with status: pending and no acceptance criterion for legajo extraction`\n  1117|> `the existing endpoint contract is preserved unchanged; no new field was added`\n  1118|> `NFR-04 keeps the source's 19:00-06:00 value and the 23:00 variant is not modelled`\n  1119|\n  1120|Then set `blocking: true` with the reason in `why_it_matters`. A run that shipped an artifact\n  1121|proceeded on something; name it specifically.\n  1122|\n  1123|**One sentence per row — reused text is a defect.** If two rows in the same sidecar share an\n  1124|`assumption_if_unanswered` string, at least one of them is wrong: two different unknowns\n  1125|cannot have been proceeded on in the same way. Before emitting, check that the values are\n  1126|distinct and that each names its own ids. A row is finished when a reader who has not seen\n  1127|the artifact could tell, from that field alone, what the document says about that item today.\n  1128|\n  1129|Observed 2026-09-05 (`product-discovery__pli-20260905-1jcnazb__pd-001`): ten of eleven\n  1130|blocking rows carried the identical sentence \"the field is left pending and no value was\n  1131|assumed\" — an earlier revision of this section offered it as an example and it was copied\n  1132|verbatim. That is not a cosmetic problem. When the operator bulk-accepts a row, the ledger\n  1133|records `assumption_if_unanswered` AS THE ANSWER (`answered_by: operator`,\n  1134|`note: \"Bulk-accepted by the operator\"`). Ten questions were therefore settled with an\n  1135|answer of record that says nothing, and no future answer can be compared against them.\n  1136|\n  1137|**Registries without those columns — derive, do not blank.** Gap tables\n  1138|(`coverage_gaps`, `alignment_gaps` and similar) typically carry only something like `id | type | ref_id | description |\n  1139|affected_section` or `id | origin | description | impact_on_boundaries`. Every sidecar field\n  1140|is still derivable, and every one is still required:\n  1141|\n  1142|| sidecar field | where it comes from when the standard column is absent |\n  1143||---|---|\n  1144|| `id` | the table's OWN id column — `GAP-01`, `AG-01`, `ASM-01`. Never drop it: an entry with no id cannot be referenced, folded, or answered against |\n  1145|| `question` | the `description` / `assumption` text. A statement of absence (\"hosting platform is not identified\") is a valid question — keep the wording, do not invent a new one |\n  1146|| `kind` | `assumption` for any assumptions table, `question` for everything else |\n  1147|| `subject` | `affected_section`, `section`, or the registry's own name |\n  1148|| `why_it_matters` | `impact_on_boundaries`, `impact_if_wrong`, `rationale`, or the consequence implied by `type` (e.g. `deferred_by_v3.0_scope` → what is therefore unplanned) |\n  1149|| `traces_to` | `ref_id`, `affected_ids`, or the ids named in the description |\n  1150|| `blocking` | decided per the rule below; these rows are usually `false` |\n  1151|| `assumption_if_unanswered` | **SYNTHESIZE it.** These tables have no `fallback_behavior` column, and the field is still required on every `question`. State what the artifact did in its absence — \"no hosting provider is assigned to any service\", \"the dependency is recorded with owner unassigned\". Omitting it is the one thing you may not do |\n  1152|| `artifact_ref` | your own artifact's project-relative path + `#open-questions` |\n  1153|\n  1154|**An assumption already confirmed is a decision, not an open question.** If an\n  1155|`assumptions_to_validate` row carries `confidence: confirmed`, `validation_needed: none`, or\n  1156|a rationale saying the point is settled, it does not belong in `_open_questions` — it will\n  1157|sit in the operator's inbox asking them to decide something the artifact says is already\n  1158|decided. Emit only assumptions that genuinely still need validating.\n  1159|\n</file>","isError":false,"durationMs":11}}
