[08:51:15] {"type":"tool_start","payload":{"toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-94966425f3216dd1","toolName":"read","args":{"path":"C:\\IA Trainings\\CalcService\\context-pack\\system\\execution-protocol.md","offset":1160,"limit":200}}}
[08:51:15] {"type":"tool_end","payload":{"toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-94966425f3216dd1","toolName":"read","result":"<file path=\"C:\\IA Trainings\\CalcService\\context-pack\\system\\execution-protocol.md\" lines=\"1541\" start=\"1160\" end=\"1359\" returned=\"200\" has_more=\"true\" truncated=\"false\">\n  1160|Observed 2026-09-05 (`discovering-bounded-contexts`, `pli-20260905-1jcnazb`): all five\n  1161|registries were correctly mirrored, and the five rows from the four non-`pending_inputs`\n  1162|tables arrived with `id`, `kind`, `subject`, `why_it_matters`, `traces_to` and `artifact_ref`\n  1163|all null — `GAP-01`, `AG-01`, `UG-01`, `UG-02` lost their ids entirely, two rows carried no\n  1164|`assumption_if_unanswered` at all, two more shared one string, and a `confidence: confirmed`\n  1165|assumption was raised as an open question. Mirroring the table is half the job; mapping its\n  1166|columns is the other half.\n  1167|\n  1168|**A mostly-blocking sidecar means you copied the column.** In the registry table\n  1169|`blocking: yes` means \"this document is not complete\"; in the sidecar it means \"downstream\n  1170|steps must not run,\" which stops a playlist and needs an operator to confirm. Those diverge\n  1171|in practice: most rows whose `fallback_behavior` names a real behavior the artifact proceeded\n  1172|on are `blocking: false` here, however the table marks them. So treat a high blocking rate as\n  1173|a self-check, not a result — if most of your rows come out `true`, re-read the column\n  1174|semantics rather than shipping it. Observed 2026-09-05: eleven of fourteen rows arrived\n  1175|`blocking: true`, `blocking_asserted_by: sidecar`, and every one was waived by the operator\n  1176|in a single bulk action.\n  1177|\n  1178|**`audience` is decided per row, not defaulted per artifact.** The test is who is ENTITLED to\n  1179|decide, and for a technical artifact that is frequently NOT the client. A tooling choice\n  1180|(\"Flyway or Liquibase for the versioned DDL?\"), a policy the delivery team owns (\"does\n  1181|architecture accept the absence of backend authorization?\"), a contract shape your own\n  1182|downstream architecture step will settle — those are `architect`. Scope, priority and\n  1183|what \"done\" means are `product_owner`. `client` is for facts about their business, their\n  1184|data, their policy or their commercial choice; it is the only value that sends a question out\n  1185|of the building, and over-using it stalls questions your own next step could answer. A useful\n  1186|check: if the question is one your own architecture or delivery capability will have to\n  1187|answer anyway, it was never `client`.\n  1188|\n  1189|A skill whose registries differ from this shape names its own mapping in its §11.5 callout.\n  1190|A skill whose registries match it needs no instruction beyond naming them.\n  1191|- `inherited_from` — the ids of questions this one restates, copied verbatim from the `## OPEN QUESTIONS` / `## DECISIONS` / `## PROJECT DECISIONS` blocks (`[OQ-1d0efb0c]` → `\"OQ-1d0efb0c\"`). This is what folds a restatement onto the row already tracking it instead of opening a second one. See **Your `## Open Questions` section lists what THIS run raised** below.\n  1192|\n  1193|**`kind` separates a gap from a claim.** A `question` names something UNKNOWN — the operator resolves it by supplying the value, or by accepting the assumption you proceeded on. An `assumption` names something you ASSERTED and could not verify (\"Theobald is compatible with the SAP S/4HANA landscape\", \"Fabric is licensable in Germany West Central\") — the operator resolves it by confirming it, which changes nothing because the artifact already says it, or by correcting it, which invalidates everything built on it. Emit `assumption` for anything you would have written into an `assumptions_to_validate` table. A question phrased as a statement of absence (\"No client-approved RPO exists\") is still a `question`: it needs a value supplied, not a claim confirmed. For an `assumption` you may omit `assumption_if_unanswered` — the claim IS what the work proceeded on.\n  1194|\n  1195|**`blocking` means the SCHEDULE, not the document.** Registry tables use `blocking: yes` to mean \"this document cannot be considered complete.\" Here it means \"downstream steps must not run until this is answered,\" and an operator has to confirm before it stops anything. If work can proceed on a stated assumption, it is `false` with an `assumption_if_unanswered` — that is the normal case.\n  1196|\n  1197|**Completeness.** The sidecar is complete by contract: list EVERY open question this run is raising, or write `[]`. Do not emit a partial list — the orchestrator reads an unlisted question as one you resolved and stops tracking it — and do not omit the key: an absent key is not \"nothing raised\", it is a missing required output.\n  1198|\n  1199|**Say what you proceeded on.** `assumption_if_unanswered` is required on every\n  1200|`question`, and it is the field that decides what answering COSTS.\n  1201|\n  1202|With it, an answer that agrees with what you assumed settles the question and\n  1203|touches nothing. Without it, there is no basis to compare an answer against, so\n  1204|EVERY answer must be treated as invalidating — and each one puts every artifact\n  1205|built on the gap into the operator's repair queue, including the answers that\n  1206|merely confirm what the document already said.\n  1207|\n  1208|Field evidence (2026-08-27): eleven open rows on one ledger, none with an\n  1209|assumption. Five of them re-asked something an earlier lap had already raised\n  1210|WITH an assumption and the operator had already settled. One — a tie-break rule\n  1211|for identical values — existed five times, had been answered twice by the\n  1212|client, and came back a fifth time with the assumption dropped, so the\n  1213|established answer could not be matched against it.\n  1214|\n  1215|If you genuinely could not proceed, that is not a reason to omit the field. Say\n  1216|what you did instead — \"the row is emitted with the field absent and flagged\" —\n  1217|because that IS what you proceeded on. Do not write a refusal token (`BLOCKED`,\n  1218|`TBD`, `N/A`, `unknown`) into it: that says you stopped, not what you assumed,\n  1219|and it renders downstream as \"proceed on: BLOCKED\", which is worse than leaving\n  1220|the question open. If work truly cannot continue, set `blocking: true` and put\n  1221|the reason in `why_it_matters`.\n  1222|\n  1223|**Deferring to a later phase still names an interim baseline.** Registering a\n  1224|gap against a future phase (\"Ideation\", \"Discovery\", \"Foundation\") instead of\n  1225|answering it now is a legitimate outcome when the information genuinely is not\n  1226|available yet — but the deferral itself is not what you proceeded on; the\n  1227|phase name alone tells a downstream agent nothing to build against today. The\n  1228|preferred pattern is `Deferred to <phase>; interim baseline: <the concrete\n  1229|state a downstream agent can build against right now>` — e.g. \"Deferred to\n  1230|Discovery; interim baseline: scope excluded from Phase 0\" or \"Deferred to\n  1231|Foundation; interim baseline: proceed with the industry-standard 99.9% SLA\n  1232|target.\" This is the same rule as above, phrased for the deferral case: the\n  1233|prohibited value is not `BLOCKED` alone, it is any deferral with no baseline —\n  1234|\"Deferred to Discovery\" and nothing else is a refusal token wearing a phase\n  1235|name.\n  1236|\n  1237|**Every question names an owner.** `audience` is required, and it is a decision\n  1238|rather than a formality: it separates a technical call the delivery team makes\n  1239|from a domain question only the client can settle. Standard delivery asks the\n  1240|client early and waits — an engagement cannot do that if nobody said which\n  1241|questions were theirs.\n  1242|\n  1243|Choose by who is ENTITLED to decide, not who is likely to reply:\n  1244|\n  1245|- `client` — a fact about their business, their data, their policy, their\n  1246|  commercial choice. The only value that sends a question out of the building.\n  1247|- `product_owner` — scope, priority, what \"done\" means for this engagement.\n  1248|- `architect` — a technical decision with real trade-offs, ours to make.\n  1249|- `internal` — anything the delivery team settles among itself.\n  1250|\n  1251|**Never use `internal` as a default.** It is what a record gets when the field is\n  1252|absent, so an omitted `audience` is indistinguishable from a considered \"we will\n  1253|handle this\" — and it means \"do not send it out\". Field evidence (2026-08-27):\n  1254|across two live ledgers, 318 of 353 rows and 85 of 85 were `internal`, and\n  1255|almost every one was a skill that simply never declared it. On the second\n  1256|project the operator's client-questions list was empty while the client owned a\n  1257|dozen of them, so the questions that most needed asking were the ones nobody\n  1258|could see.\n  1259|\n  1260|When you genuinely cannot tell, `product_owner` is the right hedge: it puts the\n  1261|row in front of a human who can route it instead of burying it.\n  1262|\n  1263|**A template field is not a question.** An open question is a DECISION the project\n  1264|needs that the inputs did not supply. It is not the absence of a value in a template\n  1265|column. Agent-native templates carry descriptive fields on every item — `usage_frequency`\n  1266|on a persona, `measurement_method` on a KPI, `owner` on a dependency, `likelihood` and\n  1267|`impact` on a risk, `complexity` on an epic — and a brief rarely speaks to any of them.\n  1268|Registering each empty one as a pending input fills the operator's inbox with rows nobody\n  1269|will answer and buries the handful of real unknowns among them.\n  1270|\n  1271|Field evidence (2026-09-08, `fs-training` / `product-definition`): 59 rows from one PRD,\n  1272|51 of them shaped `<section>/<item>: what is the value of \\`<field>\\`? (missing)`;\n  1273|seventeen shared five identical assumptions (\"No usage frequency assumed\" on six personas,\n  1274|\"No owner assumed\" on seven dependencies, …); 33 were tagged `client`, asking the customer\n  1275|for the value of a template column. The genuine unknowns — thresholds, award amounts, a\n  1276|points formula — were eight rows out of fifty-nine, and the prompt budget dropped 41 rows\n  1277|before the next step saw them.\n  1278|\n  1279|The rules, in order:\n  1280|\n  1281|1. **The decision test.** Raise a question only when a downstream artifact would be BUILT\n  1282|   DIFFERENTLY depending on the answer AND the inputs do not contain it — acceptance\n  1283|   criteria of an MVP requirement, a threshold an NFR commits to, an amount, a formula, a\n  1284|   provider. A descriptive field the inputs never mention is written as `not stated in\n  1285|   inputs` in the artifact and the item keeps its status; it is not a pending input, and\n  1286|   it is not an assumption either.\n  1287|2. **One question per decision, never one per field.** Several items missing the SAME\n  1288|   descriptive field is at most ONE question (\"Do the personas need a usage frequency, and\n  1289|   who supplies it?\") — and usually none. Two rows with the same `assumption_if_unanswered`\n  1290|   are already a defect (above); a table where a dozen rows share one is this rule being\n  1291|   broken.\n  1292|3. **Phrase it for the owner.** \"What is the value of `tier_thresholds`?\" is a template\n  1293|   speaking. \"Which Relationship Value boundaries define the three tiers?\" is a question a\n  1294|   product owner can answer. If you cannot rephrase a row away from the column name, it\n  1295|   was not a question.\n  1296|4. **A missing template field is never `client`.** The client owns facts about their\n  1297|   business; whether a template column is filled is a delivery-team matter — `product_owner`\n  1298|   at most, and only when rule 1 passes.\n  1299|5. **Self-check before writing the registry.** If more than a third of your items would get\n  1300|   a pending-input row, or the rows would exceed ~15, you are registering template\n  1301|   completeness, not project unknowns: re-apply rule 1 and collapse under rule 2. The\n  1302|   artifact's `status` may still read `draft`; the inbox is not where completeness is\n  1303|   tracked.\n  1304|\n  1305|**A question that does not name its own consequence is not a question yet.**\n  1306|Passing the decision test (rule 1) is necessary but not sufficient — a row can\n  1307|be about a genuine decision and still fail to earn its place in the registry\n  1308|if the wording does not show WHY it is decision-bearing. Before writing a row,\n  1309|apply this screen:\n  1310|- **Names the downstream artifact it changes.** `why_it_matters` states, in\n  1311|  terms of the artifact's own ids, which FR/NFR/epic/story would be worded or\n  1312|  valued differently on each answer — not the bare impact level (\"HIGH\" is not\n  1313|  an answer; \"HIGH — FR-05 cannot be enabled in production until the window is\n  1314|  fixed\" is). A row whose consequence you cannot state this specifically has\n  1315|  not passed rule 1, it has only been asserted to.\n  1316|- **Does not presuppose an answer.** A question phrased so that only one\n  1317|  answer reads as correct is not gathering information, it is asking for\n  1318|  confirmation of a decision already made — if you already know the answer,\n  1319|  it is a `decisions_confirmed` entry or an `assumption`, not a `question`.\n  1320|- **Does not bundle independent decisions into one row.** \"What is the SLA\n  1321|  target and who owns the escalation path?\" is two decisions wearing one\n  1322|  question mark; a reader can answer one half and leave the other silently\n  1323|  unanswered. Split it, or — if only one half is actually decision-bearing —\n  1324|  drop the other half per rule 1.\n  1325|- **Is answerable by its named `audience` without reading the artifact.** If\n  1326|  answering the question requires the recipient to first go read the section\n  1327|  it came from to understand what is being asked, phrase it in the recipient's\n  1328|  terms instead of the artifact's internal vocabulary (see rule 3 above).\n  1329|\n  1330|Traceability gaps and assumptions-to-validate are unaffected: an orphan requirement and\n  1331|an unverified claim are real, and each is one row.\n  1332|\n  1333|**A status line is not a question.** `_open_questions` carries questions, not a\n  1334|report about them. \"None newly raised\", \"no new gaps identified\", a count of\n  1335|what the section holds, or a note that inherited questions are carried forward\n  1336|unchanged — none of these is something an operator can answer, and each one\n  1337|lands in the inbox as work. Say it in the artifact's prose if it is worth\n  1338|saying; emit `[]` here if this run raised nothing.\n  1339|Field evidence: \"None newly raised. PLAN-SPEC's existing open questions are\n  1340|inherited unchanged\" reached a live inbox from a `code-review` sidecar.\n  1341|\n  1342|**This includes reporting that you followed the rule above.** Having correctly\n  1343|declined to republish the questions you inherited, do NOT then write a line in\n  1344|the section — or a `note:`-prefixed entry in the sidecar — saying that you\n  1345|declined. An empty section, or no section at all, already says it. Field\n  1346|evidence (2026-09-05, the day the no-republishing rule shipped): a verification\n  1347|step emitted `note: No new questions were raised by this verification run;\n  1348|inherited product questions were not republished.` It had done exactly the right\n  1349|thing and then filed a status line about it, which the operator had to read and\n  1350|discard. The correct sidecar for that run was `\"_open_questions\": []`. If a reader genuinely needs to know the run examined the inherited\n  1351|questions and found no new gap, that belongs in the report's prose, not in a\n  1352|register of things somebody has to answer.\n  1353|\n  1354|**A harness failure is not an open question.** A pinned path that does not\n  1355|resolve, an `output_spec_source` absent on disk, a caller-supplied path that\n  1356|arrives malformed, an alias that did not resolve against the skills root\n  1357|(§16.4), a directory you could not write to — these are run bookkeeping, the\n  1358|raising-side mirror of \"Process metadata is never Blocking\" in the reviewer's\n  1359|verdict duty. They are not claims about the artifact's subject, and no operator\n</file>","isError":false,"durationMs":11}}
