[08:51:17] {"type":"tool_start","payload":{"toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-825676ca5e2642d3","toolName":"read","args":{"path":"C:\\IA Trainings\\CalcService\\context-pack\\system\\execution-protocol.md","offset":1360,"limit":185}}}
[08:51:17] {"type":"tool_end","payload":{"toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-825676ca5e2642d3","toolName":"read","result":"<file path=\"C:\\IA Trainings\\CalcService\\context-pack\\system\\execution-protocol.md\" lines=\"1541\" start=\"1360\" end=\"1541\" returned=\"182\" has_more=\"false\" truncated=\"false\">\n  1360|can answer them: \"What is the valid absolute path to the ADR collection?\" has no\n  1361|answer in the project's domain, only in the wiring that produced the wrong path.\n  1362|\n  1363|Emitting one anyway does lasting damage, because the ledger cannot tell it apart\n  1364|from a real question. It gets answered — necessarily with a non-answer — and\n  1365|that non-answer is promoted into `decisions.json`, where every downstream\n  1366|capability inherits it as settled fact about the project. When it is also marked\n  1367|blocking, it burns the verification loop's budget and escalates to a human gate\n  1368|that cannot fix it either, because the retried producer reproduces the same\n  1369|failure every lap.\n  1370|\n  1371|Instead: fail the step, put the diagnosis in the report's prose with the paths\n  1372|you actually tried, and route the defect the same way the reviewer does — a\n  1373|`#calibration:` tag, or a `report_issue` draft naming WHERE it lives (a\n  1374|`SKILL.md`, a numbered `execution-protocol.md` section, a capability YAML, or\n  1375|the Stepwise component) and WHAT the change is. Fix the class at the shared\n  1376|layer, not this one run.\n  1377|\n  1378|Field evidence (2026-09-06): a capability whose `domain_boundaries` / `adrs`\n  1379|parameters arrived as absolute paths — composed against `output_folder` into a\n  1380|doubled path — produced four such rows across two verification steps and one\n  1381|conformance step. All four reached `decisions.json`. One of them recorded \"No\n  1382|platform conformance claim is made; findings remain empty until the ADR\n  1383|collection is provided\" as a project decision; the ADR collection existed, at\n  1384|the path the harness had mangled. Three were blocking, cost two escalations to\n  1385|human gates, and none was answerable by the humans they escalated to.\n  1386|\n  1387|**Your `## Open Questions` section lists what THIS run raised — nothing else.**\n  1388|\n  1389|Your prompt carries these blocks above this one: `## PROJECT DECISIONS` (every\n  1390|human decision on the project, `[OQ-xxxxxxxx] question → value`), `## DECISIONS`\n  1391|(settled — apply them) and `## OPEN QUESTIONS` (still undecided in this run).\n  1392|None of them is yours to re-publish.\n  1393|\n  1394|1. **Do not copy the inherited questions into your artifact.** They are already\n  1395|   tracked, with their own ids, their own provenance and their own place in the\n  1396|   operator's queue. A copy in your document is read back out on completion as a\n  1397|   fresh raise, in a new piece of work — which is how one question becomes five.\n  1398|   Measured on 22 live projects: 304 of 776 multi-raise records had grown this\n  1399|   way, and operators watched their counters climb (42 → 48 → 52) while nobody\n  1400|   had asked anything new.\n  1401|\n  1402|   Where your work depends on an inherited question, proceed on the assumption\n  1403|   stated in the block, and say so in ONE sentence in the body citing the\n  1404|   bracketed id (`… assuming the seven-year retention in [OQ-1d0efb0c] …`). That\n  1405|   is a reference, not a register.\n  1406|\n  1407|2. **If the subject is under `## DECISIONS` or `## PROJECT DECISIONS`, it is\n  1408|   settled.** Apply it, cite the id, and move on. Re-raising it puts a decision the operator already made back in front of\n  1409|   them.\n  1410|\n  1411|3. **If a genuine residual gap remains, raise the GAP — not the question again.**\n  1412|   An answer of \"one desktop and one mobile viewport; 768px provisional\" leaves a\n  1413|   real question about tablet, and *that* is what to emit. Re-emitting \"viewport\n  1414|   breakpoints not stated\" reads as though the answer never arrived.\n  1415|\n  1416|4. **If you genuinely must restate one, carry its id.** Copy the bracketed id\n  1417|   verbatim into `inherited_from` (and into `id` if your section has no local id\n  1418|   of its own). Identity is derived from the question TEXT, so a restatement in\n  1419|   different words becomes a second row unless the id says otherwise. Do not\n  1420|   rebuild a carried-forward table for this — those registries are retired; the\n  1421|   prompt blocks are the carry-forward.\n  1422|\n  1423|Field evidence (2026-08-26): a step received eleven answered questions in its\n  1424|`## DECISIONS` block — including a concrete \"768px provisional\n  1425|breakpoint; evergreen browsers; automated accessibility checks\" — and raised\n  1426|\"viewport breakpoints/device matrix for responsive rendering not stated\" an hour\n  1427|later, citing nothing. On that project the same underlying question ended up as\n  1428|SEVEN rows across three capabilities, and a human answered it repeatedly, each\n  1429|time saying the previous answer had been fine. Every one of the fifteen\n  1430|still-open rows had been raised after its answer already existed.\n  1431|\n  1432|**Wording.** Copy `question` verbatim from the section you wrote. Identity is derived from the question text, so a reworded sidecar entry becomes a SECOND record instead of enriching the one already tracked.\n  1433|\n  1434|**Ordering.** No change to §11.4 — `_open_questions` is one more key in the same JSON, written at the same point.\n  1435|\n  1436|**Standalone runs.** No `## Run metadata` block means no sidecar at all (§11 trigger gate), and therefore no `_open_questions` either — this is the one case where the key is legitimately absent. The artifact still carries the section; nothing is lost.\n  1437|\n  1438|---\n  1439|\n  1440|## 16. Skill Reference File Resolution\n  1441|\n  1442|Universal — applies to every skill that ships a `references/` folder (or any\n  1443|other bundled file: `evals/`, `templates/`) and tells you, in its own SKILL.md,\n  1444|to \"Read `references/<name>.md` now.\"\n  1445|\n  1446|### §16.1 The rule — resolve relative to your own skill directory, never search for it\n  1447|\n  1448|The path in that instruction is already correct as written. It is relative to\n  1449|**the directory that contains the SKILL.md you are currently executing** — the\n  1450|same directory the harness resolved before invoking you. You already know that\n  1451|directory; do not rediscover it.\n  1452|\n  1453|- Compose the full path directly: `<this skill's own directory>/references/<name>.md`,\n  1454|  then `Read` it. **Read it directly — do not `glob`/`ls`/`find` the composed path\n  1455|  first \"to check it exists.\"** §16.2's gitignore failure hits a narrowly-scoped\n  1456|  existence check exactly as hard as a broad search: `.coda/skills/` is gitignored,\n  1457|  so `glob(\".coda/skills/<skill>/**/*\")` reports zero results even when the\n  1458|  directory is right there. Observed 2026-09-04: an `opening-pull-request` run\n  1459|  correctly derived the composed path, citing §16 by number, then ran that exact\n  1460|  glob \"to check\" before reading — got the gitignore false-negative — and only\n  1461|  then fell back to the broader searches this section forbids. The pre-check was\n  1462|  the mistake, not the fallback that followed it. Only `Read`'s own outcome is\n  1463|  evidence of whether the file is there; a `Read` failure is when §16.3 applies.\n  1464|- **Never** run a repository-wide or filesystem-wide `glob` or `find` to *locate*\n  1465|  a bundled reference file. That file's location is not unknown — it is a fixed\n  1466|  offset from a directory you are already inside. (`grep` is not listed because\n  1467|  you do not locate a file by name with it — but the §16.2 scan ban covers an\n  1468|  unscoped `grep -r /` just the same.)\n  1469|- This applies even under REPAIR/REBUILD, multi-turn resumption, or delegated\n  1470|  sub-agent execution: whoever is executing the skill's steps still knows the\n  1471|  skill's own directory and must not search for what it already has.\n  1472|\n  1473|### §16.2 Why unscoped search fails here\n  1474|\n  1475|Two failures compound into a session-hanging defect, observed three times across\n  1476|`defining-quick-story` and `implementing-quick-change`:\n  1477|\n  1478|1. **A gitignore-respecting `glob` returns zero hits even though the file\n  1479|   exists.** `.coda/skills/` is excluded in this repo's `.gitignore`. A `glob`\n  1480|   tool that honors `.gitignore` will report 0 results for a skill installed\n  1481|   under that path — that is a search-tool blind spot, not evidence the file is\n  1482|   missing.\n  1483|2. **Recovering from the zero-hit result by broadening the search, instead of\n  1484|   narrowing it, escalates to a filesystem-root scan** (e.g. `find / -iname\n  1485|   \"scope-escalation.md\"`). An unscoped `find` over the whole filesystem can run\n  1486|   for the full step timeout (1800s+ observed) before self-recovering, and it\n  1487|   fires this even *after* the file was already found via a narrower, correctly\n  1488|   scoped search — pure wasted wall time on top of the original mistake.\n  1489|\n  1490|The fix is not a better search — it is no search. §16.1 removes the need to\n  1491|search at all.\n  1492|\n  1493|**Scope note — the two bans here have different reach.** \"Never search for a\n  1494|bundled file, `glob` included\" is §16-specific: it applies because the file's\n  1495|location is already known, and because a gitignore-respecting `glob` is blind to\n  1496|`.coda/skills/`. \"Never scan the whole filesystem\" is **universal** — it holds\n  1497|for every search in every step, whatever you are looking for: no `find` without a\n  1498|path argument, and none rooted at `/`, `~` or `$HOME`. A *scoped* `find` over a\n  1499|directory you were given is fine and several skills instruct one; `glob`/`grep`\n  1500|remain the right tools for repo-scoped search outside §16's narrow case.\n  1501|\n  1502|### §16.3 If a bundled reference truly appears missing\n  1503|\n  1504|If, after applying §16.1 (composing the path directly, not searching), a `Read`\n  1505|of that composed path genuinely fails — the skill package itself is corrupted or\n  1506|incomplete, not merely unreachable via a gitignore-filtered search tool — treat\n  1507|this as a `data_unavailable` condition per §9 (non-interactive execution): record\n  1508|it and escalate/stop. Do not retry by broadening a search; a broader search\n  1509|cannot fix a missing file, and (§16.2) it can hang the session trying.\n  1510|\n  1511|\n  1512|### §16.4 Cross-skill paths pinned by the caller (`output_spec_source` and friends)\n  1513|\n  1514|§16.1 covers a reference bundled inside YOUR skill. A capability step may instead pin a path\n  1515|into a DIFFERENT skill — most often `output_spec_source`, naming the producer's template or\n  1516|SKILL.md so a review step can derive its criteria from the real output contract.\n  1517|\n  1518|**The runtime prefix in that pinned path is an alias, not a literal.** Capability YAML is\n  1519|written once and executed by several runtimes, each of which materialises skills into its own\n  1520|directory: `.agents/skills/`, `.coda/skills/`, `.kilo/skills/`, `~/.codex/skills/` have all\n  1521|been observed for the same capability. A path pinned as\n  1522|`.agents/skills/<producer>/references/<file>.md` therefore resolves, at run time, to\n  1523|\n  1524|> `<the skills root YOU were loaded from>/<producer>/references/<file>.md`\n  1525|\n  1526|You already know that root: it is the parent of your own skill directory, the same one §16.1\n  1527|tells you not to rediscover. Read the composed path directly. Treat the pinned prefix as\n  1528|naming *which skill and which file*, never *which runtime*.\n  1529|\n  1530|**A pinned path that \"does not exist\" is almost always this.** Before recording a missing\n  1531|producer spec, recompose it against your own skills root and read that. Observed 2026-09-05:\n  1532|two verification steps in one project reported their caller-pinned `.agents/skills/...` paths\n  1533|absent and fell back — one to a bundled template, one to scoring the input as failed — while\n  1534|the file was present the whole time under `.coda/skills/`. One of those runs returned FAILED,\n  1535|weighted 0, on a producer artifact it never opened.\n  1536|\n  1537|If the recomposed path also fails to `Read`, §16.3 applies: `data_unavailable`, record and\n  1538|escalate — and say which roots you composed against, so the capability can be corrected.\n  1539|\n  1540|---\n  1541|\n</file>","isError":false,"durationMs":8}}
