[08:52:13] {"type":"thinking_delta","payload":{"delta":"I have all the protocol context. §12: the PRD is a single known file (589 lines) — I already read it fully inline. Delegation not needed (single known path, already read). §10.2 extraction pass: source is 1 file, 589 lines > 200 lines → extraction pass triggers. But I've already read the full PRD into context. The extraction pass exists to avoid re-reading raw sources in Phase B. Since the PRD is the only source and I've read it fully, I can write an extraction file to be compliant, or... The rule says run extraction if any single source > 200 lines. I should create the extraction file. But that would be a third file — the skill says \"MUST verify exactly these 2 files exist and no other file was written\". Hmm, but _extractions/ is part of the protocol. The pre-flight checklist says exactly 2 files (EPICS-SPEC + EPICS-AUDIT) — but _progress.json is also written. The extraction folder is protocol-sanctioned. Actually §10.2 says extraction goes to {SPEC_FOLDER}/_extractions/. The verification in Step 5 says \"Verify NO other files were written (no batch-*.md, no per-section splits)\" — extraction files are protocol infrastructure, not spec splits. I'll write one extraction file to be safe and compliant.\n\nActually, given the PRD is already fully in my context (I read all 589 lines), writing an extraction file is somewhat redundant but the protocol requires it for traceability and Phase B reads. The PRD is 589 lines > 200 → extraction triggered. I'll write a compact extraction.\n\nAlso need to check project-brief.md for MVP scope filter (Step 5). The parameter project_brief_path is not in my parameters, but the MVP filter says \"project-brief.md (or its equivalent input)\". The brief is at artifacts/inputs/project-brief.md. I should read it to apply the MVP filter.\n\nLet me now plan the epics. PRD data:\n\nFRs: FR-01..FR-10 (10 FRs)\nNFRs: NFR-01..NFR-07\nJTBD: 4\nKPIs: 7\nPersonas: P-01..P-04\nRisks: RSK-01..04\nAssumptions: ASM-01..07\nDEPs: DEP-01, DEP-02\nLITs: LIT-01..11\n\nMVP phasing: phase_1_mvp: FR-01, FR-03, FR-04, FR-05, FR-09, FR-10; phase_2: FR-02; phase_3: FR-06, FR-08; FR-07 depends on FR-06 (phase 3 effectively).\n\nEpic candidates:\n- EPIC-01: Standard arithmetic calculation engine + calculate API (FR-01, FR-04) — core deliverable. Actually FR-04 is the API exposure; FR-01 the operations. Could be one epic \"Calculate operation API with standard arithmetic\" — demoable. Gate 1 passes.\n- EPIC-02: Deterministic high-precision computation (FR-03) — is this a discrete deliverable? It's a quality attribute of the engine. Gate 2: NFR overlap? FR-03 is an FR, not NFR. It can be demoed (same input → same result). Could be its own epic or DoD constraint on EPIC-01. Hmm. FR-03 is a functional requirement with ACs. I'd make it a feature epic or embed as DoD. Given \"deterministic, high-precision results\" is central to the value prop (JTBD-01), I'll make it a feature epic EPIC-02 (precision & determinism core) — demoable via repeated identical results.\n- EPIC-03: Input validation & fail-fast error handling (FR-05) — demoable.\n- EPIC-04: Advanced operations (FR-02) — phase 2, should_have.\n- EPIC-05: Optional history tracking with non-blocking persistence (FR-06, FR-07) — phase 3, could_have. FR-07 (must_have) is the reliability guarantee — embed as DoD on EPIC-05? FR-07 depends on FR-06. FR-07 is must_have but only meaningful when history exists. I'll include FR-07 in the history epic as a DoD constraint/trace.\n- EPIC-06: History retrieval (FR-08) — phase 3, could_have. Could merge with EPIC-05? FR-08 has pending contract (OQ-025246e3 / PI-02). Keep separate epic, status pending-ish. Actually merging FR-06 and FR-08 into one epic loses traceability? No — an epic can trace to multiple FRs. But FR-08's contract is undefined; separate epic flagged. I'll keep EPIC-05 (history recording) and EPIC-06 (history retrieval) separate for traceability.\n- EPIC-07: Extensibility of operation registry (FR-09) — is this demoable? \"New operations added without breaking existing consumers\" — it's an architectural quality. Could be enabler? Gate 3: infrastructure? It's a functional requirement (FR-09, must_have). It's demoable (add an operation, existing consumers unaffected). I'll make it a feature epic or embed as DoD on the calculation engine epic. Hmm. Extensibility is really a design constraint on how operations are implemented. I could embed as DoD constraint on EPIC-01. But it has its own AC (FR-09-AC-01). I'll make it a feature epic EPIC-04? Let me structure:\n\nEpics:\n- EPIC-01: Standard arithmetic calculation engine (FR-01) — must_have, M\n- EPIC-02: Calculate API endpoint (FR-04) — must_have, S/M. Depends on EPIC-01.\n- EPIC-03: Deterministic high-precision computation (FR-03) — must_have, M. Depends on EPIC-01.\n- EPIC-04: Fail-fast input validation and error handling (FR-05) — must_have, S. Depends on EPIC-02 (API) or EPIC-01.\n- EPIC-05: Operation extensibility without breaking consumers (FR-09) — must_have, M. Depends on EPIC-01.\n- EPIC-06: Stateless deployable horizontally scalable service (FR-10) — must_have, M. This is architectural — could be enabler? It's an FR with ACs, demoable (deploy multiple instances). Type: enabler? Gate 3: infrastructure → enabler, trace to KPI. FR-10 traces to JTBD-04 and enables KPI-01 adoption. Hmm, enablers trace to KPI not FR. FR-10 is an FR, so a feature epic covering it is fine. I'll make it a feature epic (deployment & scaling) — actually it's infrastructure. The template shows enablers with fr: —. But an enabler can trace to FR? Gate 3 says enabler traces to KPI. FR-10 is a functional requirement in the PRD, so a feature epic is legitimate. I'll keep type: feature with fr: FR-10.\n- EPIC-07: Advanced operations (FR-02) — should_have, phase_2, M. Depends on EPIC-01/02.\n- EPIC-08: Optional calculation history recording (FR-06, FR-07) — could_have, phase_3, M. Depends on EPIC-02. FR-07 must_have embedded as DoD.\n- EPIC-09: History retrieval (FR-08) — could_have, phase_3, S. Depends on EPIC-08. Status: pending (contract undefined — OQ-025246e3/PI-02).\n\nWait — MVP scope filter (Step 5): every epic must trace to a project-brief MVP bullet. I need to read project-brief.md. Phase 3 items (history) — are they in the brief's MVP scope? The brief lists History Tracking as core capability §2 (optional). The MVP filter uses \"MVP Scope\" section of the brief. Need to read the brief.\n\nAlso enabler: API contract documentation (KPI-04) — documentation → enabler. KPI-04: API contract documented for internal reuse. That's a deliverable (documentation) → Gate 3 → enabler EPIC-10: API contract documentation, traces to KPI-04. Also KPI-02 (deterministic unit test coverage) → could be DoD constraint or enabler (test infrastructure). KPI-02: 100% deterministic unit test coverage — embed as DoD on calculation epics? Or enabler \"deterministic test suite\". I'd embed KPI-02 as DoD on EPIC-01/03. Hmm, but enabler for test harness is plausible. Keep it simpler: embed as DoD.\n\nKPI-01 (at least one upstream integration) — that's adoption, external; not an epic we build; disposition deferred/cross_cutting. DEP-01 relates.\n\nLet me also consider an enabler for the embedded persistence abstraction (DEP-02)? That's part of EPIC-08.\n\nPersonas coverage: P-01 (backend dev) — EPIC-01/02; P-02 (automation engineer) — EPIC-02/06 (stateless scaling); P-03 (AI agent operator) — EPIC-02 (deterministic calc); P-04 (reporting) — EPIC-03 (determinism). All covered.\n\nNow themes:\n- T-01: Calculation Core — features: standard arithmetic (FR-01), deterministic precision (FR-03), extensibility (FR-09)\n- T-02: Service API & Operations — calculate endpoint (FR-04), error handling (FR-05), stateless deployment (FR-10)\n- T-03: Calculation History — history recording (FR-06, FR-07), history retrieval (FR-08)\n- T-04: Advanced Computation — advanced operations (FR-02)\n- T-05: Adoption Enablement — API documentation (KPI-04)\n\nDependencies:\n- EPIC-02 blocked by EPIC-01 (endpoint exposes engine)\n- EPIC-03 blocked by EPIC-01\n- EPIC-04 blocked by EPIC-02 (validation at API boundary)\n- EPIC-05 blocked by EPIC-01\n- EPIC-07 blocked by EPIC-02 (advanced ops via endpoint) — or EPIC-01. FR-02 depends_on FR-01. Use EPIC-01.\n- EPIC-08 blocked by EPIC-02\n- EPIC-09 blocked by EPIC-08\n- EPIC-10 (docs enabler) blocked by EPIC-02 (document the contract)\n- EPIC-01, EPIC-06: no blockers.\n\nTopological order: EPIC-01 → EPIC-06 (no blockers, but engine first) ... order: \n1. EPIC-01 (no blockers)\n2. EPIC-06 (no blockers)\n3. EPIC-02 (blocked by EPIC-01)\n4. EPIC-03 (blocked by EPIC-01)\n5. EPIC-05 (blocked by EPIC-01)\n6. EPIC-04 (blocked by EPIC-02)\n7. EPIC-10 (blocked by EPIC-02)\n8. EPIC-07 (blocked by EPIC-01)\n9. EPIC-08 (blocked by EPIC-02)\n10. EPIC-09 (blocked by EPIC-08)\n\nEdges: EPIC-02←01, EPIC-03←01, EPIC-04←02, EPIC-05←01, EPIC-07←01, EPIC-08←02, EPIC-09←08, EPIC-10←02 = 8 edges. All satisfied by the order above.\n\nPhase mapping: phase_1_mvp: EPIC-01, EPIC-06, EPIC-02, EPIC-03, EPIC-05, EPIC-04, EPIC-10 (docs supports KPI-04, must-have-ish). phase_2: EPIC-07. phase_3: EPIC-08, EPIC-09. deferred: batch, expression eval, workflow integration, event-based history.\n\nprd_id_ownership: all FRs (10), NFRs (7), JTBD (4), KPIs (7), RSK (4), ASM (7), DEP (2), P (4), LIT (11) = 56 rows. That's a big table but required.\n\nNFR embedding (KFM-003 — mechanical sweep):\n- NFR-01 (performance, ms latency) → DoD on EPIC-01, EPIC-02 (standard ops response). Cross-cutting.\n- NFR-02 (determinism) → DoD on EPIC-03 (and EPIC-01).\n- NFR-03 (availability: calc succeeds when persistence fails) → DoD on EPIC-08 (history epic). Mirrors FR-07 (ASM-05/OQ-abad2154 — cite).\n- NFR-04 (scalability) → DoD on EPIC-06.\n- NFR-05 (observability readiness) → DoD on EPIC-06 (deployment) — structured logging baseline per PI-05.\n- NFR-06 (security) → pending, no source evidence (OQ-8a3647b2). DoD on EPIC-02 with pending target; cite OQ.\n- NFR-07 (usability) → DoD on EPIC-02, EPIC-10 (simple payload baseline per OQ-4d202bf8).\n\nOpen questions THIS run raises: Careful — inherited OQs must not be re-raised. What does THIS run discover?\n\nCandidates:\n1. EPIC-09 (history retrieval) has no endpoint contract — that's PI-02/OQ-025246e3, inherited. Cite, don't re-raise. But the epic's status: I'll mark it pending with fallback citing OQ-025246e3. Not a new question.\n2. Theme grouping is inferred (ASM-E01 style assumption) — the grouping of FRs into themes/epics is my inference. That's a genuine assumption this run raises: \"Epic grouping inferred from PRD FR dependencies and mvp_phasing\". Register as assumption ASM-E01. That's standard per template.\n3. Whether FR-07 (must_have) should be delivered in phase_3 with FR-06 or pulled earlier — the PRD says FR-07 depends on FR-06 and both land phase_3 per mvp_phasing (FR-07 isn't listed in any phase actually! phase_1: FR-01,03,04,05,09,10; phase_2: FR-02; phase_3: FR-06, FR-08. FR-07 is missing from mvp_phasing!). Interesting — FR-07 is must_have but not assigned a phase. Sequencing note says FR-07 requires FR-06 to exist before testable. So I place FR-07 with EPIC-08 in phase_3. Is that a new question? The PRD's sequencing_notes already resolve it (FR-07 testable only after FR-06). I'll follow that and note it — no new question needed; it's derivable.\n4. KPI-01/DEP-01 first integration target — inherited OQ-87c0d49a. Cite.\n5. Uncovered personas/goals: all covered. Alignment gaps: none? EPIC-10 enabler traces to KPI-04 — aligned. No unaligned epics.\n\nSo this run's own open questions: maybe just the theme/epic grouping assumption (ASM-E01). Possibly also: \"EPIC-09 sequencing — should history retrieval stay in backlog despite pending contract?\" — that's covered by inherited OQ. I'll raise one assumption row (grouping) and maybe one question about whether the API documentation enabler should be phase_1 or phase_2? That's derivable — KPI-04 is a success criterion; docs needed for adoption (KPI-01). Keep phase_1. Don't over-raise.\n\nActually, also consider: FR-07 priority must_have but placed in phase_3 epic — a downstream planner might question. I'll note it in the epic body citing the PRD sequencing note. Not a new question.\n\nSo _open_questions sidecar: 1 assumption entry (ASM-E01 grouping). Good — minimal and honest.\n\nWait — also the MVP scope filter may defer some epics. Need to read project-brief.md first. Let me read it.\n\nAlso SESSION_ID: what to use? The PRD used pd-20261027-calc001. My _progress.json used session_id \"pd-20261028-calc001\". Run metadata session = 'product-calcservice'. §1 governs SESSION_ID format for code-development family; planning-epics isn't in that family, so no minting required. I'll use \"pd-20261028-calc001\" consistent with the tracker I already wrote. Filenames: EPICS-SPEC-pd-20261028-calc001.md and EPICS-AUDIT-pd-20261028-calc001.md.\n\nPath budget: longest path = artifacts/outputs/product-definition/epics/EPICS-SPEC-pd-20261028-calc001.md ≈ well under 240. Fine.\n\nNow read project-brief.md for the MVP filter.","turnId":"25ec5e0d-d1a6-4acc-ad76-3be8c343ce3a"}}
