[08:54:55] {"type":"thinking_delta","payload":{"delta":"Now the epics section. I have the epic plan from the ownership table:\n\n- EPIC-01: Standard arithmetic calculation core (feature, T-01, FR-01, FR-03, must_have, M)\n- EPIC-02: Calculate API contract (feature, T-01, FR-04, FR-05, must_have, M)\n- EPIC-03: Extensible operation registry (enabler, T-01, FR-09, must_have, M) — wait, FR-09 is a functional requirement; Gate 3: is it infrastructure/tooling? It's an architectural capability — could be feature. FR-09 traces to FR, so feature is fine. Actually \"extensibility\" is more of an architectural enabler but it has FR trace, so type: feature is defensible. Hmm — Gate 3 asks \"Is this documentation, infrastructure, or tooling?\" An extensible operation registry is infrastructure-ish but it delivers user-visible capability (new operations). I'll make it a feature with FR trace. Actually let me reconsider: the registry pattern is the mechanism enabling FR-09. It's demoable (add a new operation without breaking consumers). Feature works.\n- EPIC-04: Advanced operations extension (feature, T-02, FR-02, should_have, M)\n- EPIC-05: Optional history persistence (feature, T-03, FR-06, FR-07, could_have, M)\n- EPIC-06: History retrieval (feature, T-03, FR-08, could_have, S)\n- EPIC-07: Stateless deployable service shell (enabler, T-04, FR-10, must_have, M) — infrastructure → enabler, traces to KPI-01 (and FR-10). Enabler traces to KPI per gate 3. FR-10 is an FR though. Hmm, enabler with fr: FR-10 and kpi: KPI-01? The template shows enabler with fr: — and kpi: KPI-02. But FR-10 is a functional requirement owned by this epic. I'll type it as enabler (infrastructure: deployment/scaling shell) with fr: FR-10 and kpi: KPI-01. That satisfies both.\n- EPIC-08: API contract documentation (enabler, T-04, KPI-04, must_have, S) — documentation → enabler, traces to KPI-04.\n\nMVP scope filter (Step 5): every epic must trace to (a) FR/NFR in PRD and (b) an MVP bullet in project-brief.md. The brief's MVP-equivalent content: §2 Core Capabilities (standard ops, precision), §4 API contract, §5 NFGs, §7 success criteria (integration, test coverage, history persistence validated, API documented). History tracking is \"Optional\" in the brief — but it IS in the brief (§2 History Tracking, §4 History Retrieval, §7 \"Optional history persistence validated\"). The PRD phases history to phase_3. The MVP filter says items failing either trace move to deferred_candidates in AUDIT. EPIC-05/EPIC-06 trace to FR-06/FR-08 which are in the PRD and correspond to brief §2 History Tracking (Optional) + §7 success criterion. Semantic overlap exists (brief mentions history tracking/retrieval), so brief_trace passes. They're phase_3, not MVP-phase, but the filter is about brief traceability, not phase. I'll keep them canonical with brief anchors, noting phase_3.\n\nBrief anchors:\n- EPIC-01: brief §2 Standard Operations + §2 Precision Strategy\n- EPIC-02: brief §4 API Contract (Calculate Operation)\n- EPIC-03: brief §2 (extensibility is in discovery transcript; brief §1 \"controlled evolution\") — brief §1 Business Objective \"controlled evolution of numerical behavior\" + §2 Core Capabilities. OK.\n- EPIC-04: brief §2 Advanced Operations\n- EPIC-05: brief §2 History Tracking (Optional) + §7 \"Optional history persistence validated\"\n- EPIC-06: brief §4 History Retrieval (Optional)\n- EPIC-07: brief §3 Architectural Positioning\n- EPIC-08: brief §7 \"API contract documented for internal reuse\"\n\nDependencies (epic_to_epic):\n- EPIC-02 blocked by EPIC-01 (API exposes the engine) and EPIC-07? Hmm. Does the API need the service shell first? The shell is deployment infrastructure; the API can be built and demoed in-process. To keep the graph minimal and truthful: EPIC-02 depends on EPIC-01 (engine to expose). EPIC-07 (service shell) — the API runs inside it; for KPI-01 integration demo you need deployment. I'd say EPIC-02 does not strictly depend on EPIC-07 for demo (can run locally), but the stateless deployment is needed for the integration success criterion. Keep it simple: no edge EPIC-02←EPIC-07; instead EPIC-07 has no blockers, and KPI-01 integration is cross-cutting. Hmm, but sequencing should have the shell early. Let me define:\n  - EPIC-01: blocked by EPIC-03? The operation registry is the extensibility mechanism — arguably the core implements operations via the registry. FR-09 depends_on FR-01 in the PRD (extensibility preserves the contract FR-01 establishes) — so FR-01 first, then FR-09. So EPIC-03 blocked by EPIC-01. Hmm, but I described EPIC-03 as the registry the core uses. PRD says FR-09 depends_on FR-01. Follow the PRD: EPIC-03 blocked by EPIC-01.\n  - EPIC-02 blocked by EPIC-01 (FR-04 depends_on FR-01).\n  - EPIC-04 blocked by EPIC-01 (FR-02 depends_on FR-01) and EPIC-03 (advanced operations added via the registry without breaking consumers). PRD only declares FR-02→FR-01. Adding EPIC-03 as blocker is a reasonable delivery dependency (new operations are added through the extensibility mechanism). I'll include it with mitigation note — it's grounded: FR-09 is the mechanism for adding operations, FR-02 adds operations. OK.\n  - EPIC-05 blocked by EPIC-01 (FR-06 depends_on FR-01).\n  - EPIC-06 blocked by EPIC-05 (FR-08 depends_on FR-06).\n  - EPIC-07: none.\n  - EPIC-08 blocked by EPIC-02 (documents the contract EPIC-02 delivers).\n\nNo cycles.\n\nTopological order:\n1. EPIC-07 (enabler, no blockers)\n2. EPIC-01 (no blockers)\n3. EPIC-02 (blocked by EPIC-01)\n4. EPIC-03 (blocked by EPIC-01)\n5. EPIC-04 (blocked by EPIC-01, EPIC-03)\n6. EPIC-05 (blocked by EPIC-01)\n7. EPIC-06 (blocked by EPIC-05)\n8. EPIC-08 (blocked by EPIC-02)\n\nEdges: EPIC-02←EPIC-01; EPIC-03←EPIC-01; EPIC-04←EPIC-01; EPIC-04←EPIC-03; EPIC-05←EPIC-01; EPIC-06←EPIC-05; EPIC-08←EPIC-02. That's 7 epic_to_epic edges. All satisfied by the order above.\n\nPhase mapping:\n- phase_1_mvp: EPIC-07, EPIC-01, EPIC-02, EPIC-03, EPIC-08\n- phase_2: EPIC-04\n- phase_3: EPIC-05, EPIC-06\n- deferred: batch calculations, expression evaluation, workflow integration with automation agents, event-based history, multi-instance history consistency, regulatory-grade audit persistence\n\nComplexity: EPIC-01 M, EPIC-02 M, EPIC-03 M, EPIC-04 M, EPIC-05 M, EPIC-06 S, EPIC-07 M, EPIC-08 S.\n\nPriority tiers: must_have: EPIC-01, 02, 03, 07, 08; should_have: EPIC-04; could_have: EPIC-05, EPIC-06. That's 3 tiers — good (≥20% non-must).\n\nnfr_dod per epic:\n- EPIC-01: NFR-01 (millisecond-level), NFR-02 (100% determinism)\n- EPIC-02: NFR-01, NFR-02, NFR-06 (pending target), NFR-07 (simple payload baseline)\n- EPIC-03: — (none directly; KPI-02)\n- EPIC-04: NFR-02 (determinism applies to advanced ops too — reasonable, NFR-02 says \"same input always produces same result\" generally)\n- EPIC-05: NFR-03 (100% availability when persistence fails)\n- EPIC-06: —\n- EPIC-07: NFR-04 (horizontal scaling), NFR-05 (observability readiness)\n- EPIC-08: —\n\nNFR propagation sweep (KFM-003): all 7 NFRs enumerated: NFR-01→EPIC-01,02; NFR-02→EPIC-01,02,04; NFR-03→EPIC-05; NFR-04→EPIC-07; NFR-05→EPIC-07; NFR-06→EPIC-02; NFR-07→EPIC-02. Every NFR appears as DoD on ≥1 functional epic. ✓ (sweep table goes in AUDIT).\n\nPersonas per epic:\n- EPIC-01: P-01, P-04\n- EPIC-02: P-01, P-02, P-03, P-04\n- EPIC-03: P-01\n- EPIC-04: P-01, P-04\n- EPIC-05: P-01\n- EPIC-06: P-01\n- EPIC-07: P-02, P-03\n- EPIC-08: P-01, P-02, P-03, P-04\n\nAll 4 personas covered.\n\nBusiness value: must reference JTBD/KPI specifically.\n\nNow write the epics section. It's large — one Edit call. The template says one section per Edit; epics is one section. It's big but manageable.","turnId":"8567d6ae-fd3f-47e5-a98d-fee1d6d0f266"}}
